/* 10వ తరగతి – eng పూర్తి పాఠాలు (tools/split/split_class10.py) */
((window.RAAY_TBF=window.RAAY_TBF||{})[10]=window.RAAY_TBF[10]||{})["eng"]=[{"id":"en1","no":1,"t":"A Letter to God + Dust of Snow (poem)","m":[6],"ty":"Story + Poem","th":"Faith, Hope, Nature","by":"G.L. Fuentes; Robert Frost (poem)","sum":"Lencho, a poor farmer, lives in a house on the crest of a low hill. His field of ripe corn needs a downpour. The rain comes and he compares the big drops to new coins, but soon a strong wind and a huge hailstorm destroy the whole crop. Lencho has complete faith in God, so he writes a letter to God asking for a hundred pesos. The postmaster laughs at first, but is touched by Lencho's faith; he collects money from his employees and friends, gives part of his own salary, and sends seventy pesos signed \"God\". Lencho is not surprised; he counts the money, gets angry that thirty pesos are missing, and writes another letter asking God to send the rest not by mail because \"the post office employees are a bunch of crooks\". The irony: the people who helped him are called thieves. Poem – Dust of Snow: a crow shakes down a dust of snow from a hemlock tree on the poet; this small event changes his mood and saves part of a day he had regretted.","te":"లెంచో ఒక పేద రైతు. పండిన మొక్కజొన్న పంటకు వర్షం కావాలనుకున్నాడు. వాన చినుకులను \"కొత్త నాణేలు\" అన్నాడు; కానీ వడగళ్ళ వాన పంటను పూర్తిగా నాశనం చేసింది. దేవునిపై గాఢ విశ్వాసంతో \"వంద పెసోలు పంపు\" అని దేవునికే ఉత్తరం రాశాడు. పోస్ట్‌మాస్టర్ అతని నమ్మకాన్ని చూసి చలించి, ఉద్యోగుల నుండి, తన జీతం నుండి డబ్బు సేకరించి \"దేవుడు\" పేరుతో 70 పెసోలు పంపాడు. లెంచో ఆశ్చర్యపోలేదు; 30 పెసోలు తక్కువ అని కోపంతో \"మిగతా డబ్బు పోస్టాఫీసు ద్వారా పంపకు, ఉద్యోగులు దొంగలు\" అని మళ్ళీ రాశాడు – ఇదే వ్యంగ్యం (irony). కవిత: కాకి హెమ్లాక్ చెట్టు నుండి మంచు రాల్చగానే కవి దిగులు మనసు మారింది.","pts":["Story by G.L. Fuentes (Mexico); characters: Lencho, his wife, sons, the postmaster, post office employees.","Lencho compares raindrops to new coins: big drops = ten-cent pieces, little ones = fives.","Hailstones looked like \"frozen pearls\"; the field became white \"as if covered with salt\".","Two conflicts: humans vs nature (hailstorm) and humans vs humans (Lencho vs post office employees).","Irony: Lencho calls his helpers \"a bunch of crooks\".","Dust of Snow (Robert Frost) – crow and hemlock are usually symbols of sorrow/death, but here they bring joy; small things can change our mood."],"w":[["crest","top of a hill (శిఖరం)"],["downpour","heavy rainfall (కుండపోత వాన)"],["hailstones","small balls of ice (వడగళ్ళు)"],["locusts","insects that destroy crops (మిడతలు)"],["plague","a disaster"],["intimate","close, personal"],["amiable","friendly"],["conviction","firm belief"],["contentment","satisfaction"],["crooks","dishonest people, thieves"],["hemlock","a poisonous tree"],["rued","regretted (విచారించాడు)"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Storm words: cyclone, gale, typhoon, tornado, hurricane, whirlwind.","Relative clauses (which, who, whose, whom): \"Lencho prayed to God, whose eyes see into our minds.\"","Using negatives for emphasis: \"Not a single leaf remained on the trees.\" \"Never in his career had the postman seen such an address.\"","Different meanings of \"hope\": wish, polite concern, expectation, little chance (\"hoping against hope\")."]},"qa":[["What did Lencho hope for?","A good downpour (shower of rain) for his ripe corn field."],["Why did Lencho call the raindrops \"new coins\"?","Because the rain would give him a good harvest and money."],["What happened to Lencho's fields?","Hailstones destroyed the crop completely; the field looked white as if covered with salt."],["Who did Lencho have faith in?","God – he wrote a letter to God asking for a hundred pesos."],["How much money did the postmaster send?","A little more than half – seventy pesos."],["What is the irony in the story?","Lencho calls the post office employees \"a bunch of crooks\", though they gave him the money."]],"act":"Fill a money order form (workbook) and write a letter of thanks; collect stories of people with strong faith/hope.","kn":"Money order: one of the cheapest ways to send money through the post office. Robert Frost (USA) won the Pulitzer Prize four times.","qz":[["Who wrote \"A Letter to God\"?","G.L. Fuentes","Robert Frost","Liam O'Flaherty","Ruskin Bond"],["Where was Lencho's house?","On the crest of a low hill","Near a river","In a big town","In a forest"],["What did Lencho compare the raindrops to?","New coins","Frozen pearls","Salt","Diamonds"],["The hailstones looked like?","Frozen pearls","New coins","Cotton","Stones"],["How much money did Lencho ask for?","A hundred pesos","Seventy pesos","Fifty pesos","Thirty pesos"],["Who read Lencho's letter first?","The postmaster","A postman","His wife","The mayor"],["How much money did Lencho receive?","Seventy pesos","A hundred pesos","Thirty pesos","Nothing"],["Whom did Lencho call \"a bunch of crooks\"?","The post office employees","The farmers","His sons","God"],["Why did the postmaster sign the letter \"God\"?","To keep Lencho's faith in God alive","He was afraid","He liked jokes","It was the rule"],["What conflict is NOT in the story?","Man vs animals","Man vs nature","Man vs man","Faith vs reality"],["\"Dust of Snow\" is written by?","Robert Frost","Leslie Norris","John Berryman","Adrienne Rich"],["In \"Dust of Snow\", the crow shook snow from a?","Hemlock tree","Oak tree","Pine tree","Mango tree"],["\"I had rued\" means?","I had regretted","I had enjoyed","I had ruled","I had forgotten"],["A violent storm with a funnel-shaped cloud is a?","Tornado","Gale","Typhoon","Breeze"],["\"Lencho prayed to God. His eyes see into our minds.\" Join with?","whose","which","whom","who"],["\"Never in his career had the postman seen such an address\" – this uses?","A negative for emphasis","A question","A relative clause","Passive voice"]],"wb":{"mis":"Relative pronoun: \"who\" for persons (subject), \"whom\" for persons (object), \"whose\" for possession, \"which\" for things. A non-defining clause takes commas.","qz":[["\"This man cheated me. I trusted him.\" – joined?","This man, whom I trusted, cheated me.","This man, which I trusted, cheated me.","This man whose I trusted cheated me.","This man, who trusted me, cheated me."],["An extremely strong wind is a?","Gale","Tornado","Hurricane","Drizzle"],["A violent storm in the western Atlantic is a?","Hurricane","Typhoon","Cyclone","Gale"],["\"Not a single leaf remained on the trees\" means?","The trees lost all their leaves","A few leaves remained","The trees were green","Leaves were single"],["\"I hope you don't mind my saying…\" shows?","Polite concern","Strong hope","Hopelessness","Anger"],["\"I often go to Mumbai. Mumbai is the commercial capital.\" – joined with?","which","who","whose","whom"]],"pg":"1–16","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":7,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 5)","Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 6)","Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 7)","Thinking about the Text (5–6); Thinking about Language (పుట 8)","Thinking about Language – Relative clauses (పుట 9)","Using negatives for emphasis (పుట 10)","Dust of Snow – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 14)","Let's Begin 1 – Lencho's faith (discussion) (పుట 1) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Let's Begin 2 – Rearrange the jumbled sentences (correct order) (పుట 2) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading – The Trader and the Tailor: complete the summary (పుట 5) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – words with \"light\" (పుట 6) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar – Relative clauses (పుట 7) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – missing words in Lencho's letter (పుట 8) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – The Race (complete story) (పుట 10) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – Man vs Nature (పుట 10) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Lencho and the tailor: actions and qualities (పుట 12) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Description of a person (Raavi) (పుట 14) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Portrait of an inspiring person (పుట 15) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 5)",["1) Lencho hoped for a good downpour (or at least a shower) for his field of ripe corn.","2) He said the raindrops were like new coins because the rain would give him a good harvest, which would bring him money – big drops were ten-cent pieces, little ones fives.","3) The rain changed into a strong wind and very large hailstones began to fall. For an hour the hail fell; the field became white as if covered with salt, and the whole corn crop was totally destroyed.","4) His soul was filled with sadness. He felt hopeless and worried – \"Not even the locusts would have left more than this… This year we will have nothing\", and the family would go hungry."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 6)",["1) Lencho had complete faith in God (the eyes of God see everything, even what is deep in one's conscience). He wrote a letter to God asking for a hundred pesos to sow his field again and to live until the next crop.","2) The postmaster read the letter (after a postman showed it to him, laughing).","3) He first laughed, but then became serious; not wanting to shake Lencho's faith, he decided to answer the letter. He collected money from his employees and friends, gave part of his own salary, and sent the money signed \"God\"."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 7)",["OCC 1) No, Lencho was not surprised at all – such was his confidence (faith) in God.","OCC 2) He got angry because the money was less than what he had asked – only seventy pesos instead of a hundred. He thought God could not make a mistake, so the post office employees must have taken the rest.","TT 1) Lencho has complete faith in God. Sentences: \"…he had a single hope: help from God, whose eyes, as he had been instructed, see everything, even what is deep in one's conscience.\" \"Lencho showed not the slightest surprise on seeing the money; such was his confidence…\" \"God could not have made a mistake, nor could he have denied Lencho what he had requested.\"","TT 2) The postmaster sends money because he did not want to shake the writer's faith in God. He signs it \"God\" so that Lencho believes the reply came from God himself and his faith stays strong.","TT 3) No. He had such strong faith in God that he believed the money came from God; it never occurred to him that someone else could have sent it.","TT 4) Lencho thinks the post office employees have taken the rest of the money. The irony is that the very people who collected the money and helped him are called \"a bunch of crooks\" by him."]],["Thinking about the Text (5–6); Thinking about Language (పుట 8)",["TT 5) Yes, there are people like Lencho – simple, innocent, hardworking people with blind faith in God, who are sometimes ungrateful and suspicious of others. (Answers may vary – people with strong faith, unquestioning, honest.)","TT 6) Humans vs nature: Lencho vs the hailstorm that destroyed his crop. Humans vs humans: Lencho's suspicion of the post office employees – he calls them crooks.","Language I: 1) cyclone 2) gale 3) typhoon 4) tornado 5) hurricane 6) whirlwind","Language II (meanings of \"hope\"): 1) wanting something to happen and thinking it possible 2) showing concern that what you say should not upset someone (polite) 3) a feeling that something good will probably happen 4) wanting something to happen though it seems unlikely 5) thinking that this would happen (it may or may not have happened) 6) feeling that something good will probably not happen"]],["Thinking about Language – Relative clauses (పుట 9)",["1) I often go to Mumbai, which is the commercial capital of India.","2) My mother, who cooks very well, is going to host a TV show on cooking.","3) These sportspersons, whose performance has been excellent, are going to meet the President.","4) Lencho prayed to God, whose eyes see into our minds.","5) This man, whom I trusted, cheated me."]],["Using negatives for emphasis (పుట 10)",["1) Not a single leaf remained on the trees.","2) The letter was addressed to none other than God himself.","3) Never in his career had the postman seen such an address."]],["Dust of Snow – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 14)",["1) \"Dust of snow\" means fine particles of snow. The sudden shower of snow shaken down on the poet by a crow from a hemlock tree changed his mood.","2) (i) Usually poets write about birds like the nightingale, peacock, cuckoo, and trees like the oak, maple, mango. (ii) The crow and hemlock are different – the crow is black and considered a bad omen; hemlock is poisonous. (iii) They represent sorrow, gloom and death. But here they bring a positive change in his mood – Frost shows that even ordinary or unpleasant things in nature can give joy.","3) Yes (personal answer) – e.g., when I failed in a test I felt sad; a friend's kind word/my pet's play/a beautiful sunset changed my mood and gave me hope."]],["Let's Begin 1 – Lencho's faith (discussion) (పుట 1) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Lencho's story moves us because even after losing his whole crop he does not lose hope – he has complete, child-like faith in God.","His faith is admirable, but blaming the post-office people is not right: they were the ones who collected the money for him. He did so because he trusted God blindly and could not imagine that God would send less – so he suspected human beings."]],["Let's Begin 2 – Rearrange the jumbled sentences (correct order) (పుట 2) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Numbers against the sentences (top to bottom): 4, 7, 3, 5, 2, 8, 6, 10, 12, 1, 13, 11, 9","Story in order: 1. Lencho, his wife and children lived in a lonely house in a valley.","2. Their corn field was longing for water.","3. Lencho predicted it would rain and it did; they were happy.","4. But it rained heavily and a hailstorm destroyed all the corn.","5. Lencho felt desperate but believed God would help him.","6. He wrote a letter to God asking for a hundred pesos.","7. He wrote \"To God\" on the envelope and posted it.","8. The postman and postmaster laughed but were surprised by his faith.","9. The postmaster collected about 70 pesos from colleagues and sent it.","10. After some days Lencho went to the post office to receive the money.","11. He opened the letter, was happy, but found only seventy pesos.","12. He asked for pen and paper, wrote a few lines and posted the letter.","13. The letter said: \"God, I received only seventy pesos… the people at the post office are a bunch of crooks.\"","Graphic description (A–D): A. Lencho in his ripe corn field, looking at the sky for rain. B. Hailstones destroy the field. C. Lencho writes a letter \"To God\". D. Lencho angrily reads the money – only 70 pesos – and writes a second letter."]],["Reading – The Trader and the Tailor: complete the summary (పుట 5) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Once there was a tailor who was known for cheating and thievery (being light-fingered). … He went to the tailor with a piece of satin cloth. The tailor welcomed him warmly. The trader was pleased by the tailor's praise. … The tailor praised the trader to the maximum. The trader was overjoyed. The tailor recounted funny tales and the trader forgot his bet. The tailor started snipping the cloth rapidly and hid the extra pieces under his thighs. Eyes of the trader were filled with tears of joy and yet the trader kept on asking for more stories. At last, the tailor told the trader that if he told another story the coat would be too tight and he would weep tears of blood."]],["Vocabulary – words with \"light\" (పుట 6) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) light year  (b) light-duty  (c) lighthearted  (d) lightheaded  (e) light sleeper"]],["Grammar – Relative clauses (పుట 7) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(b) A dictionary is a book that/which contains meanings of words.","(c) The book is about a scientist who won the Nobel prize.","(d) What is the name of the player who hit a century?","(e) I don't like people who tell lies.","(f) What is the name of the actor who won the best actor award this year?","(g) Students who need to fill in the application should meet the principal in her office.","(h) Friends of the trader thought the tailor, who is a crook, can cheat anyone.","(i) Lencho, who is faithful, thought God would help him.","(j) Those who believe in hard work will succeed.","(k) God helps those who help themselves."]],["Editing – missing words in Lencho's letter (పుట 8) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) pesos → pesos as (… sending a hundred pesos as help)","(b) very → very grateful/thankful","(c) only → only hope","(d) has → has strengthened/increased","(e) me → me survive (… help me survive until the crop …)","(f) to → to work (… promise to work hard …)"]],["Listening – The Race (complete story) (పుట 10) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. Every evening the animals of Machlipatti used to sit under the banyan tree. 2. One day Kho-Kho, the rabbit, was feeling bored. 3. He said, \"Let's run a race.\" \"Who will run a race with you? 4. No one can run faster than you,\" said Bhalu, the bear, eating a mango. 5. Choo-Choo, the rat, shouted, \"No, he's not the fastest runner. 6. I can run faster than him.\" 7. Everyone laughed at him. 8. Then it was decided to have a race between Kho-Kho and Choo-Choo. 9. The starting point was the peepul tree and the finishing point was the neem tree. 10. Coocku, the cock, crowed to start the race. 11. Kho-Kho and Choo-Choo started running. 12. After running half the distance, Kho-Kho looked back but Choo-Choo was nowhere to be seen. 13. \"He must be far behind.\" 14. When he was near the finishing point, Choo-Choo jumped over Kho-Kho's shoulder and touched the neem tree first. 15. He shouted, \"I've won the race! I've won the race!\" 16. Everyone cheered for Choo-Choo.","(Choo-Choo had quietly sat on Kho-Kho's back – brains win over speed.)"]],["Speaking – Man vs Nature (పుట 10) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. Natural calamities: floods, cyclones (e.g., Hudhud, Michaung on the AP coast), droughts, earthquakes, tsunami (2004), landslides, heat waves. Talk about loss of crops, homes and lives, and how people help each other.","2. Sample poem: \"The river rose, the fields went down, / The storm came roaring through our town; / We cut the trees, we dammed the stream, / And Nature woke us from our dream. / Let's plant, protect and wisely share – / Then Nature too will show her care.\" (Students write their own.)"]],["Writing 1 – Lencho and the tailor: actions and qualities (పుట 12) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["How does he manage the situation? – He writes a letter to God asking for 100 pesos → strong faith, hope, courage.","Does he harm anyone by his action? – No → innocent, simple, honest.","How do others think of him? – The postmaster first laughs, then admires his faith → he is naive but sincere.","What is your judgement of him? – A hard-working, simple farmer with unshakable faith in God.","He received 70 pesos and thought 30 were stolen → blind faith in God, distrust of people, unknowingly ungrateful.","Character sketch – Lencho: Lencho is a hard-working farmer who lives with his family in a lonely house on a hill. He knows farming well and loves his field. When a hailstorm destroys his crop he is sad but not hopeless, because he has deep faith in God. He writes to God for help. He is simple and honest, but he trusts God so blindly that he calls the kind post-office employees \"crooks\".","Character sketch – The tailor: The tailor is clever, witty and cunning. He is a skilful talker who flatters people and makes them laugh. Using his jokes he makes the proud trader forget his wager and steals pieces of satin under his eyes. He is dishonest, but intelligent and confident."]],["Writing 2 – Description of a person (Raavi) (పుట 14) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Raavi is a handsome young man who usually wears full-sleeved shirts. He is an engineer in a manufacturing firm. He has a practical mind – he likes to make, fix and repair things. In his free time he watches television and goes to the movies about thrice a month. He does not like playing games, but he never misses his morning walk. Raavi is congenial and supportive; his friends and colleagues like him for his helping nature.","(Mohini – sample: Mohini is a middle-aged lady who studied music and became a playback singer. She loves music and reading books. She enjoys being with people and takes part in social functions. She is firm and has a clear purpose in life, though her busy schedule leaves her no time to exercise.)"]],["Project – Portrait of an inspiring person (పుట 15) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Interview a person (teacher, doctor, farmer, sarpanch …) of ZPHS TV పల్లి / గూడూరు area. Note: childhood, education, job, likes and dislikes, contribution to the village, special qualities. Collect a photo, write a portrait in paragraphs (process approach: brainstorm → outline → draft → revise → proofread → final) and present it in class."]]]},{"id":"en2","no":2,"t":"Nelson Mandela: Long Walk to Freedom + A Tiger in the Zoo (poem)","m":[6,7],"ty":"Autobiography (extract) + Poem","th":"Freedom, Courage, Equality","by":"Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela; Leslie Norris (poem)","sum":"On 10 May 1994 Nelson Mandela was sworn in as the first Black President of South Africa at the Union Buildings, Pretoria, before dignitaries from more than 140 countries. In his speech he called the end of apartheid the end of \"an extraordinary human disaster\" and the birth of \"a glorious human achievement\" – a society in which all people are free and equal. Jets flew past, generals who once would have arrested him saluted him, and both national anthems were sung. Mandela remembers the sacrifices of thousands of patriots like Oliver Tambo and Walter Sisulu and says the \"depths of oppression\" created \"heights of character\". Courage is not the absence of fear but the triumph over it; people learn to hate, and they can be taught to love. Every man has twin obligations – to his family and to his people; in apartheid South Africa a man could not fulfil both. His hunger for his own freedom became a hunger for the freedom of his people; the oppressor too must be liberated, because he is a prisoner of hatred. Poem – A Tiger in the Zoo: the poet contrasts a caged tiger, pacing in a concrete cell, ignoring visitors and staring at the stars, with how it should live – hiding in the long grass near the water hole and terrorising villages in the jungle.","te":"1994 మే 10న నెల్సన్ మండేలా దక్షిణాఫ్రికా మొదటి నల్లజాతి అధ్యక్షుడిగా ప్రమాణం చేశాడు. 140 దేశాల నాయకులు వచ్చారు. జాతి వివక్ష (అపార్థీడ్) \"అసాధారణ మానవ విపత్తు\" ముగిసి అందరూ స్వేచ్ఛగా, సమానంగా జీవించే సమాజం ఏర్పడింది. ఒకప్పుడు అరెస్ట్ చేసే సైన్యాధికారులు సెల్యూట్ చేశారు; రెండు జాతీయ గీతాలు పాడారు. \"ధైర్యం అంటే భయం లేకపోవడం కాదు, భయాన్ని జయించడం\"; \"ద్వేషం నేర్చుకుంటే ప్రేమ కూడా నేర్పవచ్చు\". ప్రతి మనిషికి రెండు బాధ్యతలు – కుటుంబం, ప్రజలు. తన స్వేచ్ఛ కోసం ఆకలి తన ప్రజల స్వేచ్ఛ కోసం ఆకలిగా మారింది; అణచివేసేవాడు కూడా ద్వేషానికి ఖైదీ. కవిత: బోనులో ఉన్న పులి అడవిలో స్వేచ్ఛగా ఎలా ఉండాలో కవి పోల్చి చెప్పాడు.","pts":["Inauguration: 10 May 1994, Union Buildings amphitheatre, Pretoria (made of sandstone). Deputy Presidents: Thabo Mbeki and F.W. de Klerk.","10 May is an autumn day in South Africa because it is in the Southern Hemisphere.","Apartheid = political system that separates people by race.","Freedom fighters named: Oliver Tambo, Walter Sisulu, Chief Luthuli, Yusuf Dadoo, Bram Fischer, Robert Sobukwe.","\"Twin obligations\": to family and to his people/country.","A Tiger in the Zoo (Leslie Norris): contrast between the tiger in the cage and in the wild; theme – animals should be free."],"w":[["apartheid","racial segregation (జాతి వివక్ష)"],["inauguration","formal ceremony to begin a term"],["emancipation","freedom from restrictions"],["array","impressive display"],["bedecked","decorated"],["despised","hated"],["resilience","ability to recover from hardship"],["oppression","cruel and unjust treatment (అణచివేత)"],["transitory","not permanent"],["curtailed","reduced"],["prejudice","strong dislike without reason"],["wrought","done, achieved"],["brilliance","brightness"],["vivid","bright"],["rippling","moving like waves"],["menace","threat"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Noun forms with -ation/-ment/-ion: contribute → contribution, assist → assistance, subjugate → subjugation, humiliate → humiliation, imprison → imprisonment, govern → government, form → formation.","The with proper nouns: \"the Oliver Tambos\" = people like Oliver Tambo.","Idioms: \"out of breath\", \"turn my back on\", \"pushed to our limits\", \"a twilight existence\".","Balanced sentences: \"courage was not the absence of fear, but the triumph over it\"."]},"qa":[["When and where did the inauguration take place?","On 10 May 1994, in the amphitheatre of the Union Buildings, Pretoria."],["Why was 10 May an \"autumn day\" in South Africa?","South Africa is in the Southern Hemisphere, so May falls in autumn."],["What does courage mean to Mandela?","Not the absence of fear, but the triumph over it."],["What are the \"twin obligations\"?","Obligations to family (parents, wife, children) and to his people, community and country."],["Is the oppressor free, according to Mandela?","No, he is a prisoner of hatred, locked behind the bars of prejudice and narrow-mindedness."],["Where should the tiger be, according to the poem?","In the jungle, hiding in the long grass near the water hole, terrorising the villages."]],"act":"Write a short biography of a freedom fighter (Gandhi, Martin Luther King Jr.); discuss \"Freedom is indivisible\".","kn":"Nelson Mandela (1918–2013) – spent 27 years in prison (Robben Island); Nobel Peace Prize 1993 (with F.W. de Klerk). \"Long Walk to Freedom\" is his autobiography.","qz":[["When did Mandela become President of South Africa?","10 May 1994","26 January 1950","15 August 1947","10 May 1990"],["Where did the inauguration take place?","Union Buildings, Pretoria","Robben Island","Cape Town","Johannesburg"],["How many countries' leaders attended?","More than 140","About 20","50","200"],["\"An extraordinary human disaster\" refers to?","Apartheid","A flood","A war","An earthquake"],["Who was the first Deputy President?","Thabo Mbeki","F.W. de Klerk","Oliver Tambo","Walter Sisulu"],["Why were two national anthems sung?","To show equality and unity of blacks and whites","By mistake","For fun","Because of the rule"],["According to Mandela, courage is?","The triumph over fear","The absence of fear","Physical strength","Anger"],["Which comes more naturally to the human heart?","Love","Hate","Fear","Greed"],["The greatest wealth of South Africa, says Mandela, is?","Its people","Diamonds","Gold","Minerals"],["A man who takes away another's freedom is a?","Prisoner of hatred","Free man","Brave man","Leader"],["Mandela joined the?","African National Congress","Indian National Congress","Labour Party","Union Army"],["\"A Tiger in the Zoo\" is written by?","Leslie Norris","Robert Frost","John Berryman","Ogden Nash"],["In the zoo, the tiger is locked in a?","Concrete cell","Wooden house","Forest","Bamboo hut"],["At night the tiger stares at the?","Brilliant stars","Moon only","Visitors","Water hole"],["Noun form of \"imprison\"?","Imprisonment","Imprisonation","Imprisoner","Imprisonly"],["Noun form of \"humiliate\"?","Humiliation","Humiliment","Humility only","Humiliate"]],"wb":{"mis":"-ation/-ment/-ion suffixes change verbs to nouns: assist → assistance (not assistment). \"Freedom is indivisible\" = no one is free while others are oppressed.","qz":[["Noun form of \"contribute\"?","Contribution","Contributement","Contributance","Contributing"],["Noun form of \"assist\"?","Assistance","Assistion","Assistment","Assistation"],["Noun form of \"subjugate\"?","Subjugation","Subjugment","Subjugance","Subjugity"],["\"Out of breath\" means?","Breathing quickly after effort","Dead","Very happy","Silent"],["\"The Oliver Tambos of our time\" means?","People like Oliver Tambo","The Tambo family","Only Oliver Tambo","Tambo's children"],["Verb form of \"rebellion\"?","Rebel","Rebellious","Rebelled","Rebellion"]],"pg":"17–30","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":23,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 17)","Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 20)","Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 23)","Thinking about Language I (పుట 24)","A Tiger in the Zoo – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 29)","Let's Begin – Freedom and responsibility (పుట 17) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading – Sardar Patel's speech (MCQ) (పుట 21) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – sentences and word web (పుట 22) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar – Articles (పుట 24) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – error in each line (పుట 25) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – the coin dropping (notes) (పుట 26) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – What freedom means to me / assembly speech (పుట 26) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Interview with Nelson Mandela (పుట 28) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Comparative study: Gandhi and Mandela (పుట 29) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Two freedom leaders of different nations (పుట 30) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 17)",["1) The ceremonies took place in the campus (amphitheatre) of the Union Buildings in Pretoria. Indian buildings made of red sandstone: Rashtrapati Bhavan, Parliament House, Red Fort, Jama Masjid (Delhi), Humayun's Tomb.","2) South Africa lies in the Southern Hemisphere, where seasons are opposite to those in the Northern Hemisphere – May is autumn there.","3) \"An extraordinary human disaster\" means apartheid – the policy of racial discrimination under which blacks were oppressed and denied rights. The \"glorious human achievement\" is the establishment of the first democratic, non-racial government in which all are equal.","4) He thanks the international leaders for coming to witness the inauguration and for supporting the people of South Africa – a common victory for justice, peace and human dignity.","5) Ideals: liberating all people from poverty, deprivation, suffering, gender and other discrimination; a society where all are equal; \"Never, never and never again shall this beautiful land experience the oppression of one by another\"; \"Let freedom reign.\""]],["Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 20)",["1) The military generals saluted Mandela and pledged their loyalty. Earlier they would have arrested him. Their attitude changed because apartheid ended and a democratic government was freely and fairly elected.","2) Two national anthems were sung – \"Nkosi Sikelel–iAfrika\" by whites and \"Die Stem\" by blacks – to show equality, respect and unity of both races.","3) (i) In the first decade of the 20th century, the whites patched up their differences and created a system of racial domination against the blacks – one of the harshest, most inhuman societies. (ii) In the final decade, that system was overturned and replaced by one that recognised the rights and freedoms of all people regardless of skin colour.","4) To Mandela, courage was not the absence of fear but the triumph over it. The brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers that fear.","5) He thinks love is natural – \"love comes more naturally to the human heart than its opposite\". People must learn to hate; if they can learn to hate, they can be taught to love."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 23)",["OCC 1) Obligations to his family (parents, wife, children) and obligations to his people, community and country.","OCC 2) As a boy, freedom meant running in the fields, swimming in the stream, roasting mealies and riding bulls. As a student, it meant staying out at night, reading what he pleased and going where he chose. These \"transitory freedoms\" were personal; the \"basic and honourable freedoms\" were achieving his potential, earning his keep, marrying and having a family – freedom not to be obstructed in a lawful life.","OCC 3) No. The oppressor is a prisoner of hatred, locked behind the bars of prejudice and narrow-mindedness; he is robbed of his humanity, just like the oppressed.","TT 1) Because it was the end of apartheid and the beginning of a democratic, non-racial government; leaders came to show support. It signified the triumph of justice, peace, human dignity and equality over racial discrimination.","TT 2) He means he is not alone responsible; he is the result of the struggle and sacrifices of all the African patriots (Tambo, Sisulu, Luthuli…) who fought before him. That long line now continued with him.","TT 3) Yes. Oppression made men like Oliver Tambo, Walter Sisulu, Chief Luthuli strong, courageous and generous; they risked their lives, stood up to torture without breaking. Examples: Mahatma Gandhi, Bhagat Singh, Martin Luther King Jr., Alluri Seetharama Raju.","TT 4) As a boy he thought he was born free. As a student he wanted transitory personal freedoms. As a young man he wanted basic freedoms to earn and have a family. Then he realised his people were not free, and his hunger for his own freedom became a hunger for the freedom of his people – he understood that freedom is indivisible and the oppressor must also be freed.","TT 5) It turned a frightened young man into a bold one, a law-abiding attorney into a \"criminal\", a family-loving husband into a man without a home, a life-loving man into one who lived like a monk. He joined the ANC and spent many years in prison."]],["Thinking about Language I (పుట 24)",["Noun–verb pairs from the text: inauguration–inaugurate, formation–form, government–govern, demonstration–demonstrate, domination–dominate, obligation–oblige, transformation–transform, rebellion–rebel, constitution–constitute, discrimination–discriminate, achievement–achieve, deprivation–deprive.","Paragraph: contribution, assistance, subjugation, humiliation, imprisonment, agitation, alienation (Martin Luther King's… → contribution, assistance, subjugation, humiliation, imprisonment)."]],["A Tiger in the Zoo – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 29)",["1) Words describing the tiger in the cage: \"stalks the length of his cage\", \"quiet rage\", \"locked in a concrete cell\", \"his strength behind bars\", \"ignoring visitors\", \"stares at the brilliant stars\". In the wild: \"lurking in shadow\", \"sliding through long grass near the water hole\", \"snarling around houses at the jungle's edge\", \"baring his white fangs, his claws, terrorising the village\".","2) The words \"should be\" (He should be lurking… He should be snarling…) and \"quiet\" (velvet quiet / quiet rage) are repeated – the repetition emphasises where the tiger really belongs and its helpless, silent anger in the cage; it also adds rhythm.","3) Zoos help conserve endangered species and are useful for education, but animals lose their freedom and natural behaviour. Alternatives: national parks, wildlife sanctuaries, biosphere reserves, documentaries.","Contrast: in the zoo he is helpless and angry; in the wild he is free, powerful and fearless. Rhyme scheme: abcb (cage – rage, cell – well)."]],["Let's Begin – Freedom and responsibility (పుట 17) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Freedom does not mean doing whatever we like. A free citizen must respect the freedom of others, obey laws, pay taxes, vote, protect public property and help the weak. Mandela says that he could not be free while his people were not free – so freedom brings responsibility towards family, community and the nation."]],["Reading – Sardar Patel's speech (MCQ) (పుట 21) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 (b) a convocation address at a university","Q2 (a) we fail to protect our freedom","Q3 (a) to make the country strong and united","Q4 (a) character","Q5 (b) Group of nations which wage war against others","Q6 (b) India's natural resources and population are a great resource","Q7 The greatest danger is our disunity (the poison of communalism). (The options printed in the book repeat Q6 – write this answer.)","Q8 The first and foremost duty of the young nation is to make the country strong and united, and to consolidate the freedom we have won through character, discipline and mutual cooperation."]],["Vocabulary – sentences and word web (పుట 22) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. (a) Precincts: Mobile phones are not allowed within the precincts of the examination hall.","(b) Comity of nations: India is respected in the comity of nations for its peaceful policies.","(c) Unassailable: Our team took an unassailable lead of four goals.","(d) Mutual cooperation: Villages develop through mutual cooperation of the people.","2. Freedom – liberty, independence, choice, rights, dignity, equality, self-rule, democracy, free speech.","Responsibility – duty, discipline, obligation, accountability, commitment, service, honesty, sacrifice, law-abiding.","Sentences: Freedom is not free.","Freedom gives us the right to choose our own path.","Every right comes with a duty.","A disciplined citizen protects the freedom of others.","Responsibility means being accountable for our actions.","We must serve the nation with honesty and commitment."]],["Grammar – Articles (పుట 24) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. The weapons of the World War were … As a result of the churning of the ocean, the world has emitted poison. … The countries which were free … it is hard to do the same. … The freedom that we have won is yet to be consolidated.","2. \"The\" occurs most often because the nouns are specific/already known (the World War, the ocean, the world, the freedom we have won). It cannot be replaced by \"a/an\" because that would make the nouns general or indefinite and change the meaning.","3. (a) A boy and a girl … The boy … the girl","(b) the light","(c) a taxi … the station","(d) a police station","(e) a bank","(f) a job","(g) an apple","(h) a month","(i) a newspaper … a magazine. The newspaper … the magazine","(j) a good restaurant"]],["Editing – error in each line (పుట 25) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["peoples → people; is → are (clear headed and are therefore …)","wrote → write","was → is","forced → force","makes → making","problems → problem (an algebra problem)","do → does","seems → seem","writer → writers","trying → try","meaned → meaning (“sometime” – meaning when they retire …)","(last line is correct)"]],["Listening – the coin dropping (notes) (పుట 26) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Notes: walking back from lunch → crossing the street → heard a coin drop → several people turned → a woman had dropped a one-rupee coin → the tinkling sound grabs attention → whatever its value, no one can ignore it → made the writer think about sounds.","Recreated text: While returning from lunch and crossing the street, the writer heard the sound of a coin falling. Many people turned to look. A woman had dropped a one-rupee coin. The tinkling of a coin on the pavement always catches attention, whatever its value, and this made the writer think about sounds."]],["Speaking – What freedom means to me / assembly speech (పుట 26) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Outline: freedom = right to think, speak, study and live with dignity; won by sacrifice; must be protected by duty, discipline and unity.","Speech: Good morning to our HM, teachers and friends. Today I feel proud that we live in a free country, and grateful to the freedom fighters. We are politically free, but we are truly free only when there is no poverty, illiteracy, untidiness or discrimination. Are we serious about our duties? We must keep our school and village clean, respect girls and elders, save water and electricity, vote wisely and study sincerely. Let us remember Sardar Patel's words – character and discipline make a nation strong. Jai Hind!"]],["Writing 1 – Interview with Nelson Mandela (పుట 28) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1. Where and when were you born? – I was born on 18 July 1918 in Mvezo, South Africa.","Q2. What made you fight against apartheid? – I saw my people treated as inferior because of their colour; I realised that I could not be free while they were not free.","Q3. What did you lose in the struggle? – I spent about 30 years in prison and could not fulfil my duties as a son, husband and father.","Q4. What did you learn about courage? – The brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers that fear.","Q5. What is your message to the youth? – Love can be taught; no one is born hating another. Work for freedom of both the oppressed and the oppressor."]],["Writing 2 – Comparative study: Gandhi and Mandela (పుట 29) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Mahatma Gandhi","Nelson Mandela","Born 1869, Porbandar (India)","Born 1918, Mvezo (South Africa)","Lawyer; fought racial discrimination in South Africa, then British rule in India","Lawyer; fought apartheid in South Africa","Satyagraha – truth and non-violence","ANC; mainly non-violent struggle, long imprisonment","Led Salt March, Quit India Movement","Spent 27 years in prison (Robben Island)","India became free in 1947","First black President of South Africa in 1994","Simple life, self-reliance (khadi)","Forgiveness and reconciliation after apartheid","Called \"Father of the Nation\"","Called \"Madiba\"; Nobel Peace Prize 1993","Both: courage, non-violence, sacrifice, love for people, honesty and great leadership."]],["Project – Two freedom leaders of different nations (పుట 30) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Sample: Nelson Mandela (South Africa) and Ho Chi Minh (Vietnam) / Martin Luther King Jr. (USA). Write their profiles in chronological order with the prompts – thoughts, uniqueness, honesty, leadership, inspiring speeches (\"I Have a Dream\", \"Long Walk to Freedom\") and their contribution to freedom."]]]},{"id":"en3","no":3,"t":"Two Stories about Flying + How to Tell Wild Animals + The Ball Poem","m":[7,8],"ty":"Stories + Poems","th":"Courage, Self-confidence, Growing up","by":"Liam O'Flaherty; Frederick Forsyth; Carolyn Wells; John Berryman","sum":"I. His First Flight: a young seagull is afraid to fly though his brothers and sister have flown. His family scold and ignore him; he stays alone on a ledge, hungry. His mother picks up a piece of fish and flies towards him, stopping just short. Maddened by hunger, he dives at the fish, falls into space, and in terror spreads his wings – and flies. He lands on the green sea and his family praise him. II. Black Aeroplane: a pilot flying an old Dakota from Paris to England at night, dreaming of breakfast at home, enters huge black storm clouds. His compass and instruments fail; fuel is low. A mysterious black aeroplane without lights appears and guides him to the runway. When he asks the control tower about the other plane, the woman says there was no other aeroplane in the sky that night. Who helped him remains a mystery. How to Tell Wild Animals (Carolyn Wells) – a humorous poem describing how to identify the Asian lion, Bengal tiger, leopard, bear, hyena, crocodile and chameleon. The Ball Poem (John Berryman) – a boy loses his ball into the harbour; the poet says he must learn the \"epistemology of loss\" – how to bear loss and stand up.","te":"మొదటి కథ: చిన్న సముద్రపు గల్ (seagull) ఎగరడానికి భయపడింది. ఆకలితో ఉన్నప్పుడు అమ్మ చేపను దగ్గరికి తెచ్చి ఆగింది; చేప కోసం దూకి, భయంతో రెక్కలు విప్పగానే ఎగిరింది. రెండో కథ: పాత డకోటా విమాన పైలట్ రాత్రి తుఫాను మేఘాల్లో చిక్కుకున్నాడు, పరికరాలు పనిచేయలేదు; దీపాలు లేని నల్ల విమానం దారి చూపించి కాపాడింది – కానీ ఆ రాత్రి ఆకాశంలో మరో విమానం లేదని కంట్రోల్ టవర్ చెప్పింది (రహస్యం). కవిత 1: అడవి జంతువులను గుర్తించడం గురించి హాస్య కవిత. కవిత 2: బంతి పోగొట్టుకున్న బాలుడు నష్టాన్ని భరించడం నేర్చుకోవాలి.","pts":["His First Flight – Liam O'Flaherty (Ireland). Theme: self-confidence; parents push children to overcome fear.","Black Aeroplane – Frederick Forsyth. Plane: old Dakota; Paris → England; \"I'll take the risk\"; ending is a mystery.","How to Tell Wild Animals – Carolyn Wells; humour, pun (\"Bengal Tiger… noble wild beast greets you\"; \"if he hugs you… it's a bear\").","The Ball Poem – John Berryman; \"epistemology of loss\" = knowing what loss is and how to bear it.","Simple past and past perfect are used for narration."],"w":[["ledge","narrow shelf of rock"],["plateau","high flat land"],["derision","mockery, ridicule"],["cajole","persuade gently"],["shrilly","in a high, sharp voice"],["beckon","call by signal"],["swoop","move down quickly"],["monotonous","dull, without change"],["cockpit","pilot's seat area"],["compass","instrument showing direction"],["runway","path for take-off and landing"],["complexioned","with skin colour"],["hyena","wild dog-like animal"],["chameleon","lizard that changes colour"],["harbour","place where ships stay"],["epistemology","knowledge (of how to bear loss)"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Simple past vs past perfect: \"When I reached the station, the train had left.\"","Phrasal verbs / idioms: \"took a deep breath\", \"took the risk\", \"beckoning\", \"cajoling\".","Pun & humour in poems: \"he will roar at you… noble wild beast\" / hug = bear.","Use of \"will\" and \"would\" in narration."]},"qa":[["Why was the young seagull afraid to fly?","He thought his wings would not support him and he would fall into the sea."],["How did his mother make him fly?","She held a piece of fish just out of his reach; he dived at it, fell and then spread his wings."],["What was the pilot dreaming about?","Breakfast with his family in England."],["Why did the pilot follow the black aeroplane?","His instruments and radio had failed and fuel was low; the black plane guided him safely."],["What was strange about the black aeroplane?","It had no lights, and the control tower said no other plane was flying that night."],["What should the boy learn in \"The Ball Poem\"?","The epistemology of loss – to accept loss and stand up."]],"act":"Write a short humorous composition on your first attempt at learning a skill (cycling/swimming); group presentation on migratory birds or models of aeroplanes.","kn":"Seagulls are sea birds; Dakota (DC-3) is an old propeller aircraft. Chameleons change colour for camouflage.","qz":[["Who wrote \"His First Flight\"?","Liam O'Flaherty","Frederick Forsyth","Carolyn Wells","John Berryman"],["Why was the young seagull alone on the ledge?","He was afraid to fly","He was sick","He was punished for stealing","He was lost"],["What made the young seagull fly at last?","Hunger and the fish his mother brought","His father's order","A storm","His brother's push"],["Where did the young seagull finally land?","On the green sea","On the cliff","On a boat","On the beach"],["Who wrote \"Black Aeroplane\"?","Frederick Forsyth","Liam O'Flaherty","Ruskin Bond","Robert Frost"],["The pilot was flying an old?","Dakota","Boeing","Jet","Helicopter"],["Where was the pilot flying from?","Paris","London","Delhi","New York"],["What did the pilot decide when he saw storm clouds?","To take the risk and fly through","To go back to Paris","To land in a field","To jump with a parachute"],["What happened to the instruments in the clouds?","The compass and radio stopped working","They worked well","They caught fire","They fell off"],["The control tower said that night there were?","No other aeroplanes","Two aeroplanes","Many black planes","Only helicopters"],["In \"How to Tell Wild Animals\", the animal that hugs you is the?","Bear","Lion","Tiger","Hyena"],["Which animal changes colour?","Chameleon","Crocodile","Leopard","Hyena"],["The Ball Poem is written by?","John Berryman","Carolyn Wells","Leslie Norris","Adrienne Rich"],["Where did the boy's ball go?","Into the water (harbour)","Into a garden","On the road","Into the forest"],["\"Epistemology of loss\" means?","Learning how to bear loss","Buying a new ball","Crying loudly","Forgetting everything"],["\"When I reached the airport, the plane ___ left.\"","had","has","have","was"]],"wb":{"mis":"Past perfect (had + V3) shows the earlier of two past actions: \"The plane had landed before I arrived.\" Do not use past perfect for a single past action.","qz":[["\"His family ___ (fly) away before he tried.\" – correct form?","had flown","has flown","flew","flying"],["\"Derision\" means?","Mockery","Praise","Fear","Hunger"],["\"Cajole\" means?","Persuade gently","Scold","Push","Ignore"],["In the poem, the hyena is identified by its?","Laughing / smile","Stripes","Spots","Tears"],["The crocodile is identified by its?","Tears","Hug","Roar","Colour change"],["\"I'll take the risk\" shows the pilot was?","Brave but careless","Very careful","Afraid","Sleepy"]],"pg":"31–44","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":37,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["His First Flight – Thinking about the Text (పుట 35)","Black Aeroplane – Questions; Thinking about Language (పుట 39)","How to Tell Wild Animals – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 44)","The Ball Poem – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 46)","Let's Begin – His First Flight (MCQ, True/False) (పుట 32) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading – All about a Dog: story in sequence (పుట 36) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – adverbs and prefixes (పుట 37) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar A – Phrasal verbs (పుట 39) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar B – Passive voice (పుట 40) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar C – Unscramble (Amelia Earhart) (పుట 40) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – capitals, commas, full stops (పుట 41) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – Riddles (river crossing) (పుట 42) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Article: Rules in spirit, not only in letter (పుట 42) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Story: My first bicycle ride (పుట 43) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Riddles from elders (పుట 44) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["His First Flight – Thinking about the Text (పుట 35)",["1) The young seagull was afraid to fly because he felt his wings would not support him; when he ran to the edge of the ledge to fly, the great expanse of sea stretched down beneath, and he was afraid. His parents' derision and his siblings' flights made him more afraid.","2) Yes. Parents push their children when they are scared – teaching them to walk, swim, ride a bicycle. Children need encouragement and sometimes a push to overcome fear.","3) They threatened and cajoled him so that he would overcome his fear and learn to fly; otherwise he would starve. Flying was necessary for his survival.","4) Yes (personal) – e.g., my father encouraged me to learn swimming though I was afraid of water; with his support I learned.","5) Success was not guaranteed; it was important to try regardless of the possibility of failure – trying gives confidence and experience."]],["Black Aeroplane – Questions; Thinking about Language (పుట 39)",["1) The risk was flying the old Dakota into the huge black storm clouds with little fuel. He took it because he wanted to reach home in England in time for breakfast with his family; he did not want to go back to Paris.","2) Inside the storm it was very dark; he could see nothing outside; the aeroplane jumped and twisted in the air; the compass turned round and round and was dead; the radio and other instruments failed; he was lost and frightened, with fuel for only about ten minutes.","3) He had a terrible experience – the old aeroplane had almost no fuel, its instruments failed and he nearly died in the storm. He was relieved to be safe and did not want to fly that plane again.","4) When he asked who the pilot of the black aeroplane was, she said there were no other aeroplanes in the sky that night – only his plane was on the radar. So she looked at him strangely.","5) It may have been a ghost, an angel or God who helped him, or his own subconscious mind/presence of mind – it remains a mystery. (Discuss)","Language I (\"black\"): 1) dirty 2) angry/hostile look 3) most evil/shameful 4) comedy about unpleasant/serious subjects 5) black market – illegal trade 6) badly beaten (bruised)","Language II (\"fly\"): 1) Fly a flag – display a flag on a long pole 2) Fly into a rage – become suddenly very angry 3) Fly along – move quickly/suddenly 4) Fly high – be successful 5) Fly the coop – escape from a place"]],["How to Tell Wild Animals – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 44)",["1) No, \"dyin'\" does not really rhyme with \"lion\"; but if we pronounce \"lion\" as \"lyin'\" it rhymes. The poet uses poetic licence.","2) If a large, tawny beast roars at you \"dyingly\", it is the Asian Lion; if a noble wild beast with black stripes on a yellow ground greets you and eats you, it is the Bengal Tiger. You can identify them only when they attack you!","3) No. Correct spelling is \"leapt\" and \"leap\". The poet spells them \"lept\" and \"lep\" to rhyme with \"spotted\" lines (leopard – \"lep-ard\") and to create humour – poetic licence.","4) A bearhug is a strong, tight hug. A bear hugs you very hard – it can kill you; the poet humorously says this is how to recognise a bear.","5) Correct form: \"A novice might be nonplussed.\" The poet's line is better because it fits the rhythm and rhyme (\"nonplus\" – \"thus\") and adds humour.","6) Poets take liberties for rhyme and rhythm; examples: \"Dust of Snow\" (\"rued\"); humorous Telugu poems like children's rhymes, Gurajada's/Jandhyala's humour (answers may vary).","7) Humour comes from the word play – \"dyingly\", \"noble wild beast\", \"lept/lep\", \"a novice might nonplus\", \"if he roars at you as you're dyin'\" – and from the idea that you recognise an animal only when it attacks you. Rhyme scheme: abcbdb."]],["The Ball Poem – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 46)",["1) The poet does not intrude because the boy must learn by himself the \"epistemology of loss\" – how to bear loss. Another ball will not help; money cannot buy back what is lost (memories), and he has to learn responsibility.","2) Yes, he had the ball for a long time; it is linked to the memories of all the happy days he spent playing with it. So he stares down \"all his young days\" into the harbour.","3) \"In the world of possessions\" means in the world where people own things and lose them; possessions are not permanent – we will lose many things in life.","4) It seems he has not lost anything earlier – this is his first loss. Words: \"first responsibility\", \"he senses first responsibility in a world of possessions\".","5) He is learning the \"epistemology of loss\" – how to live with loss, accept it, and \"stand up\"; money is external, and every one will lose things and must learn to bear it.","6) Personal paragraph – e.g., \"I lost my favourite watch gifted by my grandfather. I felt very sad as it reminded me of him. Slowly I accepted the loss and learned to take care of my things.\""]],["Let's Begin – His First Flight (MCQ, True/False) (పుట 32) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 (a) he felt his wings would not support him","Q2 (c) He was not accepting their request to fly with them.","Q3 (a) His mother showed him the fish and moved away from him to make him fly.","Q4 (a) He was lazy and did not want to fly. (He wanted to fly but was afraid.)","Q5 (a) False (b) True (c) True (d) True","2. Black Aeroplane – sample MCQs: 1. Where was the pilot flying to? (a) Paris (b) London ✓ (c) Rome (d) Delhi","2. What was the pilot dreaming of? (a) a holiday (b) English breakfast ✓ (c) his car (d) his house","3. Why did he fly into the storm? (a) he wanted adventure (b) he did not have enough fuel to go round ✓ (c) the tower told him (d) the other pilot ordered","4. What happened to his instruments? (a) all were dead ✓ (b) only the radio worked (c) they were fine (d) only the compass was dead","5. What did the woman in the control centre say? (a) another plane was there (b) no other aeroplane was flying that night ✓ (c) the pilot was late (d) the storm was over"]],["Reading – All about a Dog: story in sequence (పుట 36) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. Once two women and a man got into a bus on a bitterly cold night and found places to sit; the young woman carried a Pekinese dog.","2. The conductor told her to take the dog out (to the top) – those were his orders. She refused because of the cold and her cough.","3. The conductor stopped the bus and waited on the pavement; the passengers protested angrily and asked for their fares back.","4. Two or three passengers walked away; the driver and the policeman did not help the passengers.","5. Slowly the bus emptied; at last the lady went to the top with her dog and the bus moved on.","6. The engine broke down; the lady came down again. When the bus was repaired the conductor saw the dog, stopped the bus again and made her go to the top.","7. The conductor did not give up. He sent the lady with the dog away to the top.","8. The writer told him that rules should be followed in spirit, not only in letter, with goodwill and good temper. The conductor took it well and said \"Good night\" amiably."]],["Vocabulary – adverbs and prefixes (పుట 37) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. (a) angrily – He shouted angrily at the boy.","(b) vaguely – I vaguely remember my first school.","(c) indignantly – The passengers protested indignantly.","(d) threateningly – The dog growled threateningly.","(e) hurriedly – She ate her breakfast hurriedly.","2. im-: immobile, immature, impossible, impatient, impolite, imperfect, impure, immortal, immoral, impartial","3. il-: illogical, illegal, illiterate, illegible, illegitimate","in-: insecure, incorrect, incomplete, inactive, independent, inaccurate"]],["Grammar A – Phrasal verbs (పుట 39) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) walk in – enter casually: The teacher walked in quietly.","(b) walk out – leave suddenly in protest: The workers walked out of the meeting.","(c) take away – remove: Please take away these empty plates.","(d) take up – start a hobby/job: She has taken up yoga.","(e) take in – understand / deceive: The old man was taken in by the salesman.","(f) take out – remove from a place: He took out a pen from his pocket.","(g) give away – give free / reveal: She gave away her old clothes to the poor.","(h) give in – surrender: The enemy finally gave in.","(i) give up – stop / quit: My father gave up smoking.","(j) put in – spend effort/time: He put in a lot of hard work.","(k) put away – keep in its place: Put away your books after reading.","(l) put out – extinguish: The firemen put out the fire."]],["Grammar B – Passive voice (పుట 40) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) The Taj Mahal was built by Shah Jahan.","(b) The malaria parasite is transmitted by the female Anopheles mosquito.","(c) The case has been solved by the police and two persons have been arrested.","(d) Many houses and lives were destroyed by the volcanic eruption in Mt. Etna.","(e) The ultraviolet rays are prevented by the ozone layer from entering the earth's surface.","(f) The field has been converted into a playground."]],["Grammar C – Unscramble (Amelia Earhart) (పుట 40) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. Amelia Earhart was an American aviator who set many flying records.","2. She became the first woman to fly solo across the Atlantic Ocean.","3. During a flight to circumnavigate the globe in July 1937, Earhart disappeared somewhere over the Pacific.","4. Her plane wreckage was never found and she was officially declared lost at sea.","5. Her disappearance remains one of the greatest unsolved mysteries of the twentieth century."]],["Editing – capitals, commas, full stops (పుట 41) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["In some parts of our country, there are frequent reports about Unidentified Flying Objects (UFOs), which people claimed to have seen flying in the sky and which are believed to be space ships carrying aliens from other planets. Some people have given evidences to support their observations. They said that they had found mysterious objects in paddy fields. Some others, after seeing a film on space, said that they had seen unusual objects flying in the sky. The public panicked. There were arguments and discussions all over the country. Police were on red alert. Many community centres were established. People became more vigilant. Gradually, the number of sightings reduced."]],["Listening – Riddles (river crossing) (పుట 42) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Riddle 1: Take the goat across first. Come back, take the wolf across and bring the goat back. Take the cabbage across (leave it with the wolf). Come back alone and take the goat across. (Or take the cabbage instead of the wolf in step 2.)","Riddle 2 (H = human, BM = big monkey, m = small monkey; 13 trips): 1. BM + m cross, BM returns. 2. BM + m cross, BM returns (both small monkeys across). 3. H + H cross, H + m return. 4. H + BM cross, H + m return. 5. H + H cross (all humans across), BM returns. 6. BM + m cross, BM returns. 7. BM + m cross – everyone is across. At no time are the humans on a bank fewer than the monkeys there."]],["Writing 1 – Article: Rules in spirit, not only in letter (పుట 42) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Rules – More than Words: Rules are made for the safety and comfort of people. Following only the words of a rule while forgetting its purpose is like the conductor in \"All about a Dog\" – he obeyed the rule but troubled everyone. We must understand why a rule exists. Traffic rules save lives, so we should wear helmets even when no police officer is watching. Laws against dowry and female infanticide protect women; families must respect daughters, not just fear punishment. Rules against cutting trees, poaching and plastic protect our environment; each citizen should plant trees and keep surroundings clean. Smoking in public places harms others. Rules work best when people follow them with self-discipline, common sense and goodwill. Schools, parents and media should create awareness, and officials should implement rules with kindness. Then rules become a way of life, not a whip."]],["Writing 2 – Story: My first bicycle ride (పుట 43) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Outline: wanted to ride like friends → afraid of falling → father held the seat → fell, hurt knee → practised every evening → father let go without telling → rode alone → joy and confidence.","Story: When I was nine, all my friends rode bicycles to school, but I was afraid. My father promised to teach me. Every evening he held the back of the seat while I pedalled on our village road. Once I fell and hurt my knee and cried that I would never learn. My father smiled and said, \"Every rider falls before he flies.\" The next day he held the seat again, but after a few metres he quietly let go. I rode to the end of the road alone, turned back and saw him clapping. Like the young seagull, I had been held back only by fear. That day I learnt that courage grows when we try."]],["Project – Riddles from elders (పుట 44) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Collect 10–15 riddles (పొడుపు కథలు) from elders, e.g., “తెల్లని పొలంలో నల్లని విత్తనాలు, చేత్తో చల్లుతారు, నోటితో ఏరుతారు” – అక్షరాలు (letters on paper). Write each riddle with its answer and translation, note why riddles were used (fun, sharpening the mind, passing on knowledge) and present them on a chart."]]]},{"id":"en4","no":4,"t":"From the Diary of Anne Frank + Amanda! (poem)","m":[8],"ty":"Diary + Poem","th":"Loneliness, Friendship, Adolescence","by":"Anne Frank; Robin Klein (poem)","sum":"Anne Frank, a thirteen-year-old Jewish girl, writes in her diary (20 June 1942) that writing a diary is a strange experience, because she has never written anything before and thinks nobody will be interested in a schoolgirl's musings. She has loving parents, a sister and about thirty friends, yet she has no true friend to confide in – so the diary will be her friend, \"Kitty\". She gives a brief sketch of her life: born in Germany (Frankfurt), the family moved to Holland in 1933 because they were Jews; her grandmother died in 1942 and she still lights a candle for her. In school she is a chatterbox. Her maths teacher, Mr Keesing, punishes her with extra homework: essays on \"A Chatterbox\", \"An Incorrigible Chatterbox\", and \"Quack, Quack, Quack, Said Mistress Chatterback\". She cleverly argues that talking is a student's trait inherited from her mother, and writes the last one as a funny poem about a mother duck and ducklings biting father swan. Mr Keesing takes the joke well and allows her to talk in class. Poem – Amanda!: a little girl is constantly nagged by an adult (mother); she escapes into a world of imagination – a mermaid, an orphan, Rapunzel – longing for freedom and peace.","te":"13 ఏళ్ళ యూదు బాలిక ఆన్ ఫ్రాంక్ తన డైరీని \"కిట్టి\" అనే స్నేహితురాలిగా భావించి రాసింది. తల్లిదండ్రులు, అక్క, ముప్పై మంది స్నేహితులు ఉన్నా మనసు విప్పి చెప్పుకునే నిజమైన స్నేహితురాలు లేదు. జర్మనీ నుండి హాలండ్‌కు వలస వెళ్ళారు (యూదులపై హిట్లర్ వేధింపులు). తరగతిలో ఎక్కువగా మాట్లాడుతుందని గణిత ఉపాధ్యాయుడు మిస్టర్ కీసింగ్ \"వాగుడుకాయ\" మీద వ్యాసాలు రాయమని శిక్షించాడు; ఆమె తెలివిగా, హాస్యంగా రాయడంతో ఆయన నవ్వి, తరగతిలో మాట్లాడనిచ్చాడు. కవిత: నిరంతరం ఆదేశాలు విని విసిగిపోయిన చిన్నారి అమాండా ఊహల లోకంలో – మత్స్యకన్య, అనాథ, రపుంజెల్ – స్వేచ్ఛ కోరుకుంటుంది.","pts":["Anne Frank (1929–1945) – Jewish girl; hid with family in a secret annexe in Amsterdam during World War II; died in a Nazi concentration camp.","Diary originally written in Dutch; published as \"The Diary of a Young Girl\" by her father Otto Frank.","Anne received the diary on her 13th birthday (12 June 1942); calls it \"Kitty\".","\"Paper has more patience than people.\"","Essays: \"A Chatterbox\", \"An Incorrigible Chatterbox\", \"Quack, Quack, Quack, Said Mistress Chatterback\".","Amanda! (Robin Klein, Australia): stanzas 2, 4, 6 in brackets show Amanda's imagination."],"w":[["confide","share secrets"],["musings","thoughts"],["downcast","sad"],["listless","without energy"],["chatterbox","one who talks too much"],["incorrigible","that cannot be corrected"],["ingenious","clever"],["emigrate","move to another country"],["unpredictable","not able to be predicted"],["dunce","slow learner"],["flounce","to move in an angry way"],["languid","slow, relaxed"],["emerald","green gem"],["tranquil","calm"],["rapunzel","girl locked in a tower (fairy tale)"],["sulk","be silently angry"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Compound words: heartbreaking, homesick, blockhead, law-abiding, overdo, daydream, breakdown, output.","Phrasal verbs: plunge in, kept on, get along with, ended up, calm down, stay in, make up for, hand in.","Idioms: \"caught my eye\", \"lost in thought\", \"a stroke of luck\", \"hand in hand\".","Contracted forms: I've = I have, I'd = I would/I had, can't = cannot, won't = will not."]},"qa":[["Why did Anne call her diary \"Kitty\"?","She wanted the diary to be her friend; she had no true friend to confide in."],["Why does Anne want to keep a diary?","She has no true friend to share her thoughts with; she feels \"paper has more patience than people\"."],["Why was Mr Keesing annoyed with Anne?","She talked too much in class; he gave her extra homework – an essay on \"A Chatterbox\"."],["How did Anne justify being a chatterbox?","Talking is a student's trait; she inherited it from her mother, who talked as much as she did."],["What made Mr Keesing allow Anne to talk?","Her funny poem \"Quack, Quack, Quack…\" – he took the joke well."],["What does Amanda yearn for?","Freedom, peace and solitude – to be left alone without constant instructions."]],"act":"Keep a diary for a week recording events; share with a partner. Prepare a list of \"do's\" a parent gives and discuss how they feel.","kn":"Anne Frank's diary has been translated into more than 70 languages. Otto Frank was the only family member who survived.","qz":[["Who wrote \"From the Diary of Anne Frank\"?","Anne Frank","Robin Klein","Otto Frank","Mr Keesing"],["What name did Anne give her diary?","Kitty","Margot","Kuperus","Amanda"],["Why did Anne feel writing a diary is strange?","She had never written anything before and thought no one would be interested","She was ill","Her teacher forbade it","She could not write"],["\"Paper has more ___ than people.\"","patience","pages","problems","power"],["Anne was born in?","Germany (Frankfurt)","Holland","Poland","England"],["Who was Anne's mathematics teacher?","Mr Keesing","Mr Smith","Mr Frank","Mr Kuperus"],["Anne's first essay was on?","A Chatterbox","A Dunce","My Family","My Diary"],["Anne's third essay was written as a?","Poem","Story","Letter","Play"],["In the poem, the swan was bitten by the?","Ducklings","Crows","Cats","Fish"],["The diary was originally written in?","Dutch","English","German","French"],["\"Amanda!\" is written by?","Robin Klein","Anne Frank","Robert Frost","Carolyn Wells"],["Amanda wants to be a mermaid to?","Drift blissfully in the emerald sea","Catch fish","Swim races","Meet friends"],["Why are stanzas 2, 4, 6 in brackets?","They show Amanda's imaginary thoughts","They are the mother's words","They are titles","They are mistakes"],["Rapunzel lived in a?","Tower","Cave","Ship","Garden"],["\"Incorrigible\" means?","Cannot be corrected","Very clever","Very quiet","Dishonest"],["\"Homesick\" means?","Missing home and family","Ill at home","Hating home","Building a home"]],"wb":{"mis":"Compound words have a combined meaning: \"blockhead\" = stupid person (not a head of a block); \"breakdown\" = machine stops working. I'd can mean \"I would\" or \"I had\" – decide from the verb after it.","qz":[["\"Law-abiding\" means?","Obeying the law","Making laws","Breaking laws","Teaching law"],["\"Blockhead\" means?","A very stupid person","A big head","A building","A wooden block"],["\"Daydream\" means?","Pleasant thoughts while awake","Sleep at night","Nightmare","Morning walk"],["\"I'd already left\" – I'd =","I had","I would","I did","I could"],["\"I'd like some tea\" – I'd =","I would","I had","I did","I should have"],["\"Plunge in\" means?","Start doing something eagerly","Swim","Fall","Leave"]],"pg":"45–56","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":51,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["Oral Comprehension Check 1 & 2 (పుట 50)","Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 53)","Thinking about Language – Compound words (పుట 54)","Phrasal verbs; Idioms (పుట 55)","Heart idioms; Contracted forms (పుట 57)","Amanda! – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 61)","Let's Begin – Anne Frank's alienation and isolation (పుట 45) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – Lena Mukhina: meanings of expressions (పుట 46) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – words, questions, MCQ (పుట 47) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – A Journal of the Plague Year: old English words (పుట 49) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – questions and MCQ (పుట 50) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – choose the word (పుట 51) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar – idioms, contractions, phrasal verbs (పుట 52) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – Max Müller quote (పుట 52) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – The Siege of Leningrad (notes) (పుట 53) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – qualities in Lena's poem (పుట 54) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Means of reporting today (పుట 54) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Report: Swachh Bharat cleanliness drive (పుట 55) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Bengal famine of 1943 (పుట 56) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["Oral Comprehension Check 1 & 2 (పుట 50)",["OCC1 1) It is a strange experience because Anne had never written anything before, and she felt that later neither she nor anyone else would be interested in the musings of a thirteen-year-old schoolgirl.","2) She has no true friend to confide in; she wants the diary to be her friend, to whom she can write her thoughts and feelings freely.","3) Because \"paper has more patience than people\" – she could not talk about anything other than everyday things with her friends; the diary would listen to everything without judging.","OCC2 1) Because nobody would understand her stories to \"Kitty\" without knowing her background; so she gives a brief sketch of her life and family.","2) She writes that she adores her grandmother; when she died in January 1942 nobody knew how often she thought of her and still loved her; on her birthday she lit a candle for her."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 3; Thinking about the Text (పుట 53)",["OCC3 1) Mr Keesing was annoyed because Anne talked too much in class. He asked her to write an essay on \"A Chatterbox\" as extra homework.","2) She argued that talking is a student's trait and she would do her best to keep it under control, but she would never be able to break the habit, since her mother talked as much as she did – one can't do much about inherited traits.","3) He was strict in giving punishments, but he was also kind and had a sense of humour – he took the joke well and allowed her to talk.","4) Her poem \"Quack, Quack, Quack, Said Mistress Chatterback\" about a mother duck and swan father; he read it to the class, took the joke in the right way and stopped punishing her.","TT 1) No. Anne was wrong; her diary became one of the most widely read books in the world, translated into many languages, because it shows the feelings of a young girl during war.","TT 2) Diary entries in \"Before You Read\" (personal journal, logbook, etc.) are records for oneself/work. Anne's diary was written in Dutch; it is different because she writes it as letters to an imaginary friend \"Kitty\", sharing her innermost thoughts.","TT 3) So that \"Kitty\" (and the reader) can understand her better. She treats Kitty as an outsider at first (giving background), but then as a close friend/insider.","TT 4) Father – \"the most adorable father\"; grandmother – loved her deeply; Mrs Kuperus – her headmistress, was sad at parting (both cried); Mr Keesing – old fogey but she manages him with wit. This shows she was loving, emotional, sensitive and intelligent.","TT 5) In her first essay she argued that talking is a student's trait and that she would try to control it, but could not break the habit as it was inherited from her mother.","TT 6) Yes; Mr Keesing was unpredictable – he was annoyed and kept punishing her, but after reading her funny poem he took it as a joke and allowed her to talk."]],["Thinking about Language – Compound words (పుట 54)",["1) Heartbreaking – producing great sadness 2) Homesick – missing home and family very much 3) Blockhead – an informal word for a very stupid person 4) Law-abiding – obeying and respecting the law 5) Overdo – do something to an excessive degree 6) Daydream – think about pleasant things, forgetting about the present 7) Breakdown – an occasion when vehicles/machines stop working 8) Output – something produced by a person, machine or organisation"]],["Phrasal verbs; Idioms (పుట 55)",["Phrasal verbs: plunge in – start doing something; kept on – continued; get along with – have a good relationship; ended up – finally; calm down – become quiet; stay in – remain at school/in class (detention); make up for – compensate; hand in – submit.","Idioms: \"noses to the grindstone\" – working very hard; \"hardly a day goes by\" – almost every day; \"can't bring myself to\" – unable to do; \"caught my eye\" – got my attention; \"lost in thought\" – thinking deeply; \"stroke of luck\" – unexpected good fortune.","\"Meet one's match\" = meet someone as good as oneself; \"let the cat out of the bag\" = reveal a secret."]],["Heart idioms; Contracted forms (పుట 57)",["Heart idioms: break somebody's heart – make very sad; close/dear to heart – very important; from the bottom of your heart – sincerely; have a heart – be kind; have a heart of stone – be cruel; your heart goes out to – feel sympathy.","Contracted forms: I've – I have; I'd – I would/I had; can't – cannot; won't – will not; don't – do not; didn't – did not; it's – it is; that's – that is; we're – we are. (\"'d\" = would or had; \"'s\" = is or has – decide by the following word.)"]],["Amanda! – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 61)",["1) Amanda is about 8–10 years old – she is told not to bite nails, not to hunch shoulders, to finish homework, tidy her room, clean her shoes, not to eat chocolate (acne).","2) Her mother (an adult/parent) is speaking to her.","3) Stanzas 2, 4 and 6 are in parenthesis because they are Amanda's private thoughts and imagination – not spoken aloud.","4) Amanda is the speaker in 2, 4 and 6. No, she is not listening to the speaker in stanzas 1, 3, 5, 7; she is lost in her own dream world.","5) As a mermaid she could drift blissfully in a languid emerald sea, free from all instructions.","6) No, Amanda is not an orphan. She imagines being an orphan so that nobody would nag her; she could roam the streets freely and write patterns in the dust.","7) Rapunzel was a girl with long golden hair locked in a tower by a witch. Amanda wants to be Rapunzel to live alone, peacefully, in a tower – \"I would never let down my bright hair\" – so no one could disturb her.","8) She yearns for freedom, peace and solitude. The poem shows Amanda is imaginative, sensitive, and tired of constant nagging.","9) The adult thinks Amanda is sulking and moody, but she is not; she is only dreaming. The last stanza shows the adult's nagging continues – \"Anyone would think that I nagged at you, Amanda!\" (irony)."]],["Let's Begin – Anne Frank's alienation and isolation (పుట 45) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Anne hid with her family in the Secret Annexe for two years; she could not go out, meet friends or even make noise. She felt lonely and had no \"true friend\", so she made her diary \"Kitty\" her friend. We can relate to her when we feel lonely in a new place, during illness or lockdown (as in the Covid days) – writing a diary or talking to someone helps us share our feelings."]],["Reading I – Lena Mukhina: meanings of expressions (పుట 46) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(b) appalling hunger and cold: terrible, shocking shortage of food and extreme cold.","(c) start of the siege: the beginning of the time when the enemy surrounded the city and cut it off.","(d) blockade grams: the very small quantity (grams) of bread given as ration during the blockade."]],["Reading I – words, questions, MCQ (పుట 47) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q2 War words: siege, air raids, artillery, bombardment, bomb shelters, bomb attacks, shells, explode, blockade, rations, Germans, enemy.","Q3 (a) The citizens of Leningrad were the captives; the city was surrounded by the Germans for 872 days.","Q3 (b) Bad weather reduced visibility, so the Germans could not bomb the city easily.","Q3 (c) They feared air raids and bombing on sunny days and moonlit nights, artillery fire, hunger, cold and death.","Q3 (d) At first they were curious, then afraid, and finally, because of hunger and cold, they became apathetic – they had reconciled to the situation, but they did not give up resisting.","Q4 (d) The possibility of buying the food without a ration card as in the past was no more than a memory now."]],["Reading II – A Journal of the Plague Year: old English words (పుట 49) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 whence → from where","whither → to which place / where","discourse → conversation, talk","tokens → signs, symptoms","vent → publicity, outlet","endeavoured → tried","concerned in → affected by","whereupon → after which","given in → reported","returned them to the Hall → reported them to the Hall","bill of mortality → list of deaths."]],["Reading II – questions and MCQ (పుట 50) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q2 (a) The rumour that the plague had returned to Holland (Amsterdam, Rotterdam) and where it came from.","Q2 (b) There were no printed newspapers; news was gathered from merchants' letters from abroad and passed on by word of mouth.","Q2 (c) The government kept its discussions very private, so the rumour died off and people hoped it was not true.","Q2 (d) When two Frenchmen died in Drury Lane, two physicians and a surgeon inspected the bodies, found clear signs of plague and declared it publicly; it was printed in the weekly bill of mortality.","Q3 (d) The list which was issued weekly, of persons who died due to plague"]],["Vocabulary – choose the word (పుట 51) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(b) oneself  (c) who  (d) yours  (e) damage  (f) concerned with"]],["Grammar – idioms, contractions, phrasal verbs (పుట 52) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. All's fair in love and war – any behaviour is acceptable in such situations: He tricked his rival, saying all's fair in love and war.","Icing on the cake – something extra that makes a good thing better: Winning the prize was good; the scholarship was the icing on the cake.","Call the shots – be in control: In our house my grandmother calls the shots.","Chalk and cheese – completely different: The twins are like chalk and cheese.","Raining cats and dogs – raining very heavily: It was raining cats and dogs, so we stayed home.","Breaking the ice – starting a conversation in an awkward situation: The teacher told a joke to break the ice.","2. (a) shouldn't (b) They've (c) Let's (d) didn't (e) couldn't (f) Here's (g) I'd (h) aren't","3. Suhasini wanted to go out with her mother to the book shop. … her car broke down on the way back. She had to look for a mechanic. She checked out several apps for roadside assistance but then found out that her internet was not working."]],["Editing – Max Müller quote (పుట 52) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["was → were","for → of (some of its choicest gifts)","Find → found","them → those","pointed → point","me → myself","has → have","want → wanted","most → more (more truly human)"]],["Listening – The Siege of Leningrad (notes) (పుట 53) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Notes: began 8 Sept 1941 → German and Finnish forces surrounded Leningrad → lasted 872 days (till Jan 1944) → constant bombing, no electricity, water, heating → bread ration fell to 125 g → about a million civilians died, mostly of starvation and cold → \"Road of Life\" across frozen Lake Ladoga brought supplies → the city never surrendered.","Narrative: On 8 September 1941 the German army cut off Leningrad from the rest of Russia. For 872 days the people lived under bombs and shells. Food, fuel and electricity ran out, and in winter the bread ration was only 125 grams a day. Hundreds of thousands died of hunger and cold. Yet the people kept working, and supplies came across the frozen Lake Ladoga. In January 1944 the Soviet army broke the siege. Leningrad became a symbol of courage and endurance."]],["Speaking – qualities in Lena's poem (పుట 54) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Qualities: curiosity / love of learning, strength, smartness, steadfastness, will power, persistence, determination, bravery, perseverance.","Example person: Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam – a newspaper boy from Rameswaram who, through hard work, will power and perseverance, became a great scientist and President of India."]],["Writing 1 – Means of reporting today (పుట 54) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Today news spreads instantly through newspapers, television news channels, radio, news websites and apps, social media (WhatsApp, X/Twitter, YouTube, Facebook), SMS alerts and government portals. Health departments publish daily bulletins and dashboards (as during Covid-19). But fake news also spreads quickly, so we must check official sources."]],["Writing 2 – Report: Swachh Bharat cleanliness drive (పుట 55) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["CLEANLINESS DRIVE IN TV పల్లి","By a Class X student, ZPHS TV Palli","TV Palli, (date): A cleanliness drive under Swachh Bharat Abhiyan was organised by the students and teachers of ZPHS TV Palli with the gram panchayat. About 120 students cleaned the school campus, the bus stop and the main road. Plastic waste was collected separately and handed over to the panchayat. Students explained the importance of using dustbins, separating wet and dry waste and avoiding single-use plastic. Slogans like \"Clean village, healthy village\" were displayed. The sarpanch appreciated the students and promised more dustbins. The drive created awareness among the villagers."]],["Project – Bengal famine of 1943 (పుట 56) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) Affected area: the Bengal province (present West Bengal, Bangladesh, parts of Odisha).","(b) Reasons: rice crop failure (cyclone, crop disease), stop of rice imports from Burma after Japanese occupation, wartime inflation and hoarding, poor government response and export/diversion of food for the war.","(c) Relief: free kitchens, relief by voluntary groups, army help after Lord Wavell (late 1943), rationing. About 2–3 million people died.","(d) Pictures/films: Chittaprosad's sketches, Satyajit Ray's film \"Ashani Sanket\". Prepare a poster titled \"Hunger amidst War\"."]]]},{"id":"en5","no":5,"t":"Glimpses of India + The Trees (poem)","m":[8,9],"ty":"Travelogue / Essays + Poem","th":"Culture and diversity of India; Nature","by":"Lucio Rodrigues; Lokesh Abrol; Arup Kumar Datta; Adrienne Rich (poem)","sum":"I. A Baker from Goa: the elders of Goa are nostalgic about the old Portuguese days and their famous loaves of bread. The bakers (paders) still exist; the thud of the baker's bamboo staff and its jingle wakes up the village. The baker wore a single-piece long frock (kabai) in Portuguese days and a shirt and half-pants in the author's childhood. Bread is a must for marriages (sweet bol), engagements, festivals and parties; cakes and bolinhas for Christmas. Monthly accounts were written on a wall with pencil. Bakers and their families never starved; their plump, jackfruit-like bodies showed it. II. Coorg (Kodagu): a heavenly district between Mysore and Mangalore with evergreen forests, coffee estates and spices; people are of Greek or Arab origin, martial and hospitable; the Coorg Regiment is decorated; General Cariappa was a Coorgi; the Kodavus may carry firearms without a licence. Rafting, canoeing, rock climbing, trekking; Brahmagiri hills, Nisargadhama, Bylakuppe (Tibetan monks). III. Tea from Assam: Pranjol and Rajvir travel by train to Assam; Rajvir knows legends about tea – the Chinese emperor Shen Nung (leaves fell into boiling water) and Bodhidharma (eyelids became tea plants). Tea was discovered in Assam in 1823. They visit the Dhekiabari Tea Estate managed by Pranjol's father. Poem – The Trees: trees in a house move out into the forest; symbolic of breaking free from confinement.","te":"1. గోవా బేకర్ (పాడర్) – పోర్చుగీస్ కాలం నుండి వచ్చిన రొట్టెల సంప్రదాయం; పెళ్ళిళ్ళు, పండుగలకు రొట్టె, కేకులు తప్పనిసరి; బేకర్ వెదురు కర్ర చప్పుడు గ్రామాన్ని లేపేది. 2. కూర్గ్ (కొడగు) – మైసూరు–మంగళూరు మధ్య అందమైన జిల్లా; కాఫీ తోటలు, సతతహరిత అడవులు; ప్రజలు గ్రీకు/అరబ్ మూలాలవారని కథనాలు; వీరులు, ఆతిథ్యం; జనరల్ కరియప్ప; రాఫ్టింగ్, ట్రెక్కింగ్; బైలకుప్పె బౌద్ధ సన్యాసులు. 3. అస్సాం టీ – ప్రాంజోల్, రాజ్‌వీర్ రైలులో అస్సాంకు; టీ పుట్టుక కథలు (షెన్ నుంగ్, బోధిధర్మ); 1823లో అస్సాంలో టీ కనుగొన్నారు. కవిత: ఇంట్లోని చెట్లు అడవిలోకి వెళ్ళిపోవడం – బంధనం నుండి విముక్తి.","pts":["A Baker from Goa – Lucio Rodrigues; \"pader\" = baker; \"kabai\" = long frock; \"bol\" = sweet bread for marriage; \"bolinhas\" = coconut cookies.","Coorg – Lokesh Abrol; Kodavus; Coorg Regiment; Field Marshal K.M. Cariappa; river Kaveri; Mahaseer fish; Bylakuppe – largest Tibetan settlement.","Tea from Assam – Arup Kumar Datta; Assam has the largest concentration of tea plantations in the world; tea first discovered there in 1823.","Legends: Chinese emperor Shen Nung (tea leaves in boiled water); Bodhidharma cut off his eyelids – ten tea plants grew.","The Trees – Adrienne Rich; conflict of man and nature / women breaking free."],"w":[["nostalgic","longing for the past"],["loaf","a shaped mass of bread"],["jingle","ringing sound"],["kabai","long frock worn by bakers"],["plump","fat, round"],["jackfruit-like","plump and healthy"],["canopy","cover of branches"],["martial","related to fighting"],["hospitality","kindness to guests"],["descent","origin, family line"],["rafting","travelling on a raft"],["sip","drink in small amounts"],["flush","growth of new tea leaves"],["legend","old traditional story"],["ruminate","think deeply"],["strain","push hard"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Collocations: unique/ancient culture; Buddhist monks; pleasant surprise; unique experience; pleasant weather.","Phrasal adjectives with up/down/out (upkeep, downpour, outbreak, breakthrough, downcast, outlook, undergo).","-ing and -ed adjectives: interesting/interested, boring/bored, exciting/excited, depressing/depressed.","Modifiers: \"coffee estates\", \"evergreen forests\", \"colonial plantations\"."]},"qa":[["What are the elders in Goa nostalgic about?","The good old Portuguese days, their famous loaves of bread and the bakers."],["Where were the baker's monthly accounts recorded?","On some wall in pencil."],["Where is Coorg?","Midway between Mysore and the coastal town of Mangalore (Karnataka)."],["What is unique about the Kodavus?","They are the only people in India permitted to carry firearms without a licence."],["When was tea first discovered in Assam?","In 1823."],["Where are the trees in the poem?","Inside the house (in pots); they move out into the forest."]],"act":"Collect information on how bread is baked now and earlier; write a travel brochure for a place you visited; find out about dying crafts (pottery, handloom) and ways to revive them.","kn":"Coffee and tea are grown on hill slopes; Darjeeling and Assam are famous for tea. Kerala and Karnataka (Coorg) are known for coffee and spices.","qz":[["\"A Baker from Goa\" is written by?","Lucio Rodrigues","Lokesh Abrol","Arup Kumar Datta","Adrienne Rich"],["What is the baker called in Goa?","Pader","Kabai","Bol","Kodavu"],["The baker's long frock was called?","Kabai","Bol","Bolinhas","Pader"],["The baker's monthly accounts were recorded?","On a wall in pencil","In a book","On paper","In a computer"],["\"Jackfruit-like appearance\" means?","Plump and healthy","Spiky","Green","Thin"],["Coorg is located between?","Mysore and Mangalore","Chennai and Bengaluru","Goa and Mumbai","Ooty and Kochi"],["The people of Coorg are possibly of ___ descent.","Greek or Arab","Chinese","African","Portuguese"],["Who among these was a Coorgi?","General Cariappa","Sam Manekshaw","A.P.J. Abdul Kalam","Bodhidharma"],["Which river flows through Coorg?","Kaveri","Krishna","Godavari","Ganga"],["Bylakuppe is famous for?","Tibetan Buddhist monks","Coffee museum","Tea","Bread"],["Tea was first discovered in Assam in?","1823","1923","1857","1700"],["Who is said to have cut off his eyelids?","Bodhidharma","Shen Nung","Rajvir","Pranjol"],["Pranjol's father manages which tea estate?","Dhekiabari","Darjeeling","Nilgiris","Munnar"],["\"The Trees\" is written by?","Adrienne Rich","Robin Klein","Robert Frost","Ogden Nash"],["In the poem, the trees move from the house to the?","Forest","River","Sky","Garden"],["Opposite feeling word: a film that makes you sleepy is?","Boring","Bored","Exciting","Excited"]],"wb":{"mis":"\"-ing\" adjectives describe the thing (an interesting book); \"-ed\" adjectives describe the feeling of a person (I am interested). Not \"I am interesting in books\".","qz":[["How do you feel when you open a present?","Excited","Exciting","Bored","Boring"],["A debate on \"Homework Should Be Banned\" is?","Interesting","Interested","Bored","Tired"],["How do you feel when it rains all day and you stay indoors?","Bored","Boring","Exciting","Interesting"],["\"A heavy ___ has been forecast.\" (heavy rain)","downpour","outbreak","upkeep","breakthrough"],["\"Scientists are on the brink of a major ___ in cancer research.\"","breakthrough","downpour","upkeep","outlook"],["Correct collocation?","Pleasant surprise","Pleasant pillow","Young age question \"How young are you?\"","Coastal cake"]],"pg":"57–74","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":63,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["A Baker from Goa – Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 63)","A Baker from Goa – Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 64)","A Baker from Goa – Thinking about the Text (పుట 65)","Coorg – Oral Comprehension Check (పుట 69)","Coorg – Thinking about Language (పుట 70)","Coorg – Complete the phrases (పుట 71)","Tea from Assam – Discuss; Thinking about Language (పుట 75)","-ing / -ed adjectives (పుట 76)","The Trees – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 79)","The Trees – Meaning of the poem (పుట 80)","Let's Begin – Five states (cuisine, dance, art) (పుట 57) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – Parsis of Delhi: meanings (పుట 59) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – questions and MCQ (పుట 60) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – Araku coffee: meanings and words (పుట 62) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – questions and MCQ (పుట 63) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – sounds (పుట 64) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 1, 2 – participles and past tense (పుట 65) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 3, 4, 5 – adjective phrases, -ing/-ed, would/used to (పుట 67) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – spelling (Valley of Flowers) (పుట 68) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – The Baker from Goa (summary) (పుట 69) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – Budget trip to Araku valley (itinerary) (పుట 70) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Account of a train journey (పుట 71) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Advertisement (పుట 72) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 3 – Breads of India (collage) (పుట 73) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Indian Railways (పుట 74) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["A Baker from Goa – Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 63)",["1) The elders in Goa are nostalgic about the good old Portuguese days, the Portuguese and their famous loaves of bread.","2) Yes. The eaters of loaves may have vanished but the makers (paders) are still there; the age-old furnaces still exist, the fire still burns and the thud and jingle of the baker's bamboo can still be heard in some places.","3) The baker is called a \"pader\" in Goa.","4) The baker came at least twice a day – once in the morning on his selling round and again when he returned after emptying his basket. Children ran to meet him for the bread-bangles (sweet loaves) which they specially liked; they did not even brush their teeth properly."]],["A Baker from Goa – Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 64)",["1) (i) marriage gifts – sweet bread called bol (ii) party or feast – bread (iii) daughter's engagement – sandwiches (iv) Christmas – cakes and bolinhas","2) (i) In the Portuguese days, a peculiar dress known as the kabai – a single-piece long frock reaching down to the knees. (ii) When the author was young, a shirt and trousers shorter than full-length and longer than half-pants.","3) Anyone who wears a half-pant reaching just below the knees invites the comment that he is \"dressed like a pader\" – because bakers wore such half-pants.","4) The monthly accounts were recorded on some wall in pencil.","5) It means a plump, round physique – showing the baker and his family were prosperous and well fed."]],["A Baker from Goa – Thinking about the Text (పుట 65)",["1) Correct: (i), (ii), (v). Not correct: (iii) (paders still exist), (iv) (they now wear shirt and half-pants), (vi) (it is not very profitable now), (vii) (they never starved).","2) Yes. Marriage gifts are meaningless without bol; a party or feast loses charm without bread; sandwiches for a daughter's engagement; cakes and bolinhas for Christmas and other festivals; the baker's furnace is still alive.","3) Tone: (i) hopeful (ii) hopeful/nostalgic (iii) nostalgic (iv) humorous (v) matter-of-fact (vi) humorous/matter-of-fact"]],["Coorg – Oral Comprehension Check (పుట 69)",["1) Coorg (Kodagu) is in Karnataka, midway between Mysore and the coastal town of Mangalore.","2) One story says a part of Alexander's army moved south along the coast and settled there when return became impractical; they married local people. Another theory says they are of Arab origin – the long black coat (kuppia) with embroidered waist-belt is like the kuffia of Arabs and Kurds.","3) (i) People: fiercely independent, of Greek or Arab descent, martial, hospitable; they love tales of valour; the Coorg Regiment is highly decorated; General Cariappa was a Coorgi; the only people allowed to carry firearms without a licence. (ii) Main crop: coffee (also spices). (iii) Sports: river rafting, canoeing, rappelling, rock climbing, mountain biking, trekking. (iv) Animals: birds, bees, butterflies, macaques, Malabar squirrels, langurs, slender loris, wild elephants, Mahaseer fish. (v) About 250–260 km from Bangalore; by air to Mangalore or Bangalore, by train to Mysore/Mangalore/Hassan, and by road (6 hours from Bangalore)."]],["Coorg – Thinking about Language (పుట 70)",["4) (i) the monsoons pour enough rain to keep many visitors away (ii) one story goes that (iii) the Coorgs are always ready to recount (tales of valour) (iv) even the most laidback individuals become converts to the life of high-energy adventure (v) the theory of Arab origin draws support from (vi) macaques and Malabar squirrels keep a watchful eye","Collocations: (a) \"How young are you?\" is odd (b) \"a pleasant pillow\" is odd. Nouns: monks – Buddhist monks, Tibetan monks; surprise – pleasant/unexpected surprise; experience – unique/thrilling experience; weather – pleasant/cold weather."]],["Coorg – Complete the phrases (పుట 71)",["(i) tales of valour (bravery) (ii) coastal town (city) (iii) a piece of heaven (land) (iv) evergreen forests (trees) (v) coffee plantations (tea plantations) (vi) rope bridge (wooden bridge) (vii) wild elephants (animals)"]],["Tea from Assam – Discuss; Thinking about Language (పుట 75)",["Discuss: Pranjol and Rajvir travel by train to Assam (Pranjol's father manages the Dhekiabari Tea Estate). Rajvir is delighted by the sea of tea bushes. Legends: Chinese emperor Shen Nung – leaves fell into his boiled water; Bodhidharma cut off his eyelids – ten tea plants grew. Tea was first discovered in Assam in 1823. Second flush (May–July) yields the best tea.","Language I.1: (i) downpour (ii) undergo (iii) upkeep (iv) dropout (v) walk-in","Language I.2: (i) overthrow (ii) breakthrough (iii) bypass (iv) outlook (v) downcast"]],["-ing / -ed adjectives (పుట 76)",["(i) interesting/exciting (ii) interesting/stimulating (iii) bored/depressed (iv) excited/surprised (v) delighted/relaxed (vi) annoyed/irritated/tired (vii) thrilled/excited (viii) interesting/touching"]],["The Trees – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 79)",["1) (i) In a treeless forest: no bird could sit, no insect could hide, the sun could not bury its feet in shadow. (ii) It creates the picture of sunlight falling on the forest floor covered with shadows of trees; the sun's \"feet\" are its rays touching the ground.","2) (i) The trees are inside the house (in pots on the veranda). Roots work all night to disengage themselves from the cracks in the veranda floor; leaves strain toward the glass; small twigs stiff with exertion. (ii) The poet compares the branches to newly discharged patients going to the clinic doors, half-dazed.","3) (i) (a) At the beginning, the moon is full and shining in the open sky. (b) At the end, the moon is broken like a mirror into pieces and shines in the crown of the tallest oak – because the trees moved out and their branches broke the moonlight. (ii) The house becomes empty and silent; the glass breaks; the smell of leaves and lichen reaches the rooms like a voice. (iii) Perhaps we do not notice or do not want to admit the changes in nature that embarrass us."]],["The Trees – Meaning of the poem (పుట 80)",["4) (i) Yes, it presents a conflict between man and nature: man has confined trees inside houses; they feel imprisoned and break out to the forest, their natural home. (ii) Trees may also symbolise women who are confined to the house and want to break free and find their own identity.","5) \"On Killing a Tree\" (Gieve Patel) – both poems speak of the strength of trees; in Rich's poem trees escape, in Patel's a tree resists being killed."]],["Let's Begin – Five states (cuisine, dance, art) (పుట 57) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Andhra Pradesh – pesarattu, Gongura pickle, Pootharekulu","Kuchipudi","Kalamkari, Kondapalli toys, Etikoppaka toys.","Kerala – appam & stew, sadya","Kathakali, Mohiniyattam","mural painting, boat race.","Punjab – makki di roti & sarson da saag","Bhangra, Giddha","Phulkari embroidery.","West Bengal – rasgulla, fish curry","Baul music, Chhau","Kantha embroidery, terracotta.","Assam – masor tenga, pitha","Bihu","Muga silk, bamboo craft. (Mark the states on an outline map of India.)"]],["Reading I – Parsis of Delhi: meanings (పుట 59) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) He persuaded Parsi visitors with sweet words to get down from the train.","(b) Jer Bai sometimes opposed bringing strangers home, but her husband did not accept her objection.","(c) Nobody ordered food from outside caterers; the women cooked everything themselves.","(d) The writer began to respect her delicate-looking grandmother because making malido needed great strength."]],["Reading I – questions and MCQ (పుట 60) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q2 (a) Nowrosji Kapadia said it at Old Delhi station to welcome Parsi passengers of the Frontier Mail and persuade them to settle in Delhi.","Q2 (b) Because Nowrosji was very hospitable and eager to build a Parsi community in Delhi.","Q2 (c) Cooking together was a tradition; the women prepared food themselves as an act of love and community service.","Q2 (d) The life of community feasts where everyone cooked and shared food and the poor never knew who paid.","Q2 (e) Making malido needed strong arms; after making it once she realised how strong her dainty grandmother was.","Q2 (f) Her grandmother dressed up for tea, and many Parsi snacks – batasa, nan-khatai, khari, patrel, kumas and bhakra – were served.","Q3 (d) Cooking malido, the Parsi style halwa.","Q4 Parsi delicacies: dar ni pori, malido, sev, ravo, mithu dahi, bafellaeeda, kheemo kaleji, dal chawal, macchi no patio, batasa, nan-khatai, khari, patrel, kumas, bhakra, dhansak, patra ni machhi, lagan nu custard.","Mithu dahi in Telugu: తియ్యని పెరుగు."]],["Reading II – Araku coffee: meanings and words (పుట 62) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 (a) moves along an unusual path / has an unusual history. (b) the drive to coffee estates that are far away and spread out. (c) hills thickly covered with forests. (d) small groups of villages.","Q2 (a) tucked (away) (b) far-flung (c) enriched (d) aroma"]],["Reading II – questions and MCQ (పుట 63) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q3 (a) It is about 1,200 m above sea level in the north-eastern corner of AP bordering Odisha, and the Araku Balloon Festival is held there every January.","Q3 (b) In the north-eastern corner of Andhra Pradesh (Alluri Sitharama Raju dist., near Visakhapatnam), bordering Odisha.","Q3 (c) \"a livelihood initiative by the Naandi Foundation to a globally appreciated product\"; \"a brand that works with 517 villages and 10,986 farmers\"; \"all of whom are estate owners and entrepreneurs with a stake in the business\".","Q3 (d) Coffee was introduced in these hills by a British civil servant, N.S. Brodie, in 1898.","Q3 (e) terrace farms – step-like fields on hill slopes","bio-dynamic farming – organic farming treating soil, plants and animals as one system","composting – making manure from waste","shade trees – trees planted to give shade to coffee","terroir – natural environment (soil, climate) where a crop grows","cherries – coffee fruits","sapling – young plant.","Q4 (a) Coffee plants are taken care of from the stage of plantation till its beans are ready for an enjoyable cup of coffee."]],["Vocabulary – sounds (పుట 64) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["shuffling of feet","striking of a match","puffing of an engine","clanking of chains","sighing of the wind","rustling of leaves","banging of the door","gurgling of water","howling of hyenas"]],["Grammar 1, 2 – participles and past tense (పుట 65) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. (a) proposed (b) watching (c) quoted (d) infected (e) taken (f) frightening  [(b), (f) need the -ing form]","2. Birds, bees and butterflies were there to give me company. Macaques, Malabar squirrels, langurs and slender loris kept a watchful eye from the tree canopy. I did, however, prefer to step aside for wild elephants. The climb to the Brahmagiri hills brought me into a panoramic view of the entire misty landscape of Coorg. A walk across the rope bridge led to the sixty-four acre island of Nisargadhama. Running into Buddhist monks from India's largest Tibetan settlement, at nearby Bylakuppe, was a bonus. The monks, in red, ochre and yellow robes, were amongst the many surprises that waited to be discovered by visitors searching for the heart and soul of India, right there in Coorg."]],["Grammar 3, 4, 5 – adjective phrases, -ing/-ed, would/used to (పుట 67) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["3. (a) I bought a sporty blue Maruti car. (b) He made a short, interesting speech. (c) Suresh went to a beautifully decorated house. (d) She bought a frightfully expensive designer dress. (e) The quickly prepared food was tasty.","4. (a) shocking, shocked (b) interesting, interested (c) excited, exciting (d) amused, amusing (e) disgusting, disgusted, disgusted","5. (a) would (b) used to (c) would / used to (d) would (e) would / used to (f) used to"]],["Editing – spelling (Valley of Flowers) (పుట 68) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["heightway → highway","varity → variety","magniÀscent → magnificent","thousand → thousands","mountain → mountains","live → leave (they leave their marks)","cigarete → cigarette","Trakking → Trekking","delikate → delicate","compeled → compelled","mountainering → mountaineering","grasing → grazing","privent → prevent"]],["Listening – The Baker from Goa (summary) (పుట 69) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Keywords: friend, companion, guide; twice a day; jingling thud of bamboo; bread-bangles; jhang-jhang; lady of the house; peep into the basket; fragrance; no brushing – tiger never brushed; hot tea.","Summary: In the writer's childhood in Goa the baker was the children's friend. He came twice a day, announcing himself with the jhang-jhang of his bamboo staff. The maid bought the loaves, but the children rushed to him for the sweet bread-bangles. Pushed aside, they still climbed on a bench to peep into his basket. They loved the fragrance of the loaves and did not even bother to brush their teeth, thinking hot tea would clean everything."]],["Speaking – Budget trip to Araku valley (itinerary) (పుట 70) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Day 1: 6:45 am Visakhapatnam → Araku by Kirandul Express Vistadome coach (≈ ₹ 700–800 per head) – 58 tunnels, Borra region views; 11:00 am reach Araku, check in at a home stay/AP Tourism Haritha resort; afternoon Tribal Museum, Coffee Museum, Padmapuram Gardens; evening Dhimsa dance.","Day 2: Morning Galikonda viewpoint, coffee plantations; Borra Caves on the way back; return to Vizag by bus/train in the evening.","Budget (per student): travel ₹ 900, stay ₹ 600, food ₹ 500, entry tickets ₹ 200 → about ₹ 2,200. Book tickets on IRCTC in advance; carry warm clothes and ID cards.","2. Tongue twisters – practise slowly, then faster."]],["Writing 1 – Account of a train journey (పుట 71) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Meanings: length and breadth – all over","chugged into – (a train) moved slowly with a puffing sound into","vouch for – confirm, guarantee","long gone – disappeared long ago","out came the treats – the snacks were brought out.","A Journey to Tirupati: Last summer our family travelled from Gudur to Tirupati, about 100 km, by the Narayanadri Express. My father booked the tickets online through IRCTC. When the train chugged into Gudur station, the platform was crowded from length and breadth. We found our reserved seats easily. The coach was clean and had fans, charging points and a pantry service. As soon as the train started, out came the treats from my mother's bag – idli and laddus. Vendors sold tea and snacks; the days of steam engines are long gone. I can vouch for the beauty of the green fields on the way. Suggestions: cleaner toilets, more drinking-water points and announcements in Telugu."]],["Writing 2 – Advertisement (పుట 72) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["ARAKU VALLEY COFFEE – \"Wake up to the Hills!\"","100% Arabica, grown by tribal farmers of Araku","Bio-dynamic, chemical-free","Rich aroma, smooth taste","250 g pack ₹ 299 – 10% off this month","Available at all leading stores & online","Call: 1800-XXX-XXXX"]],["Writing 3 – Breads of India (collage) (పుట 73) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Andhra Pradesh – pesarattu, jonna rotte, sajja rotte","Punjab – makki di roti, kulcha, paratha","Gujarat – thepla, bhakri","Maharashtra – puran poli, bhakri","Kerala – appam, Malabar parotta","Rajasthan – bati","Goa – pao, poi","Kashmir – tsot-girda, sheermal, kulcha. Paste pictures and write 2–3 sentences about each."]],["Project – Indian Railways (పుట 74) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Points: started 1853 (Bombay–Thane); one of the largest networks in the world; electrification of nearly all broad-gauge routes; Vande Bharat semi-high-speed trains, Vistadome coaches, online booking (IRCTC), bullet-train project. Luxury trains: Palace on Wheels, Maharajas' Express, Deccan Odyssey, Golden Chariot. Add pictures and travel experiences of elders."]]]},{"id":"en6","no":6,"t":"Madam Rides the Bus + The Tale of Custard the Dragon (poem)","m":[10],"ty":"Story + Poem (Ballad)","th":"Curiosity, Independence, Life and Death; Courage","by":"Vallikkannan; Ogden Nash (poem)","sum":"Valli (Valliammai), an eight-year-old girl, loves standing at her front door watching the street. Her strongest desire is to ride the bus that passes between her village and the town. By listening carefully to the neighbours and asking questions, she learns that the town is six miles away, the fare is thirty paise one way, and the journey takes forty-five minutes. She saves every coin, resisting toffees and balloons, and saves sixty paise. One afternoon, when her mother is sleeping, she boards the bus; the conductor jokingly calls her \"madam\". She behaves like a grown-up, refuses help, and enjoys the view – the canal, palm trees, green fields, a cow running in front of the bus. She does not get down in town, stays on the bus, and returns. On the way back she sees the same cow lying dead by the roadside, hit by a vehicle; she is shocked and refuses to look out of the window. At home she listens to her aunt and mother talking about death and says \"I was just agreeing…\" – keeping her adventure a secret. Poem – The Tale of Custard the Dragon (Ogden Nash): Belinda lives with a kitten Ink, a mouse Blink, a dog Mustard and a cowardly dragon Custard. All laugh at Custard for crying for a safe cage; but when a pirate comes, the others hide and Custard bravely gobbles the pirate. Still Custard keeps crying for a cage.","te":"ఎనిమిదేళ్ళ వల్లి (వల్లియమ్మై) కి బస్సు ఎక్కి పట్టణానికి వెళ్ళాలనే గాఢ కోరిక. ఇతరుల మాటలు విని, ప్రశ్నలు అడిగి – పట్టణం ఆరు మైళ్ళు, ఒకవైపు చార్జీ 30 పైసలు, 45 నిమిషాలు ప్రయాణం అని తెలుసుకుంది. మిఠాయిలు, బొమ్మలు కొనకుండా 60 పైసలు దాచింది. అమ్మ నిద్రపోతున్నప్పుడు బస్సు ఎక్కింది; కండక్టర్ \"మేడమ్\" అని ఆటపట్టించాడు. కాలువ, తాటి చెట్లు, పచ్చని పొలాలు, బస్సు ముందు పరుగెత్తిన ఆవు – అన్నీ ఆనందంగా చూసింది. తిరిగి వచ్చేటప్పుడు అదే ఆవు రోడ్డు పక్కన చనిపోయి కనిపించింది – ఆమె చాలా బాధపడి కిటికీ నుండి చూడలేదు. తన సాహసాన్ని రహస్యంగానే ఉంచింది. కవిత: పిరికి డ్రాగన్ కస్టర్డ్ దొంగ (పైరేట్) వచ్చినప్పుడు ధైర్యంగా పోరాడి అందరినీ కాపాడింది.","pts":["Story by Vallikkannan (Tamil writer); translated from Tamil.","Valli's full name: Valliammai; age: eight years.","Distance: six miles; fare: thirty paise one way (sixty paise both ways); time: forty-five minutes.","Experience of death (the dead cow) teaches Valli about life's realities.","The Tale of Custard the Dragon – Ogden Nash; ballad; characters: Belinda, Ink (kitten), Blink (mouse), Mustard (dog), Custard (dragon), the pirate.","Rhyme scheme: aabb; humorous, light-hearted; repetition and alliteration."],"w":[["pastime","hobby"],["unending","continuous"],["fare","cost of travel"],["haughtily","proudly"],["sociable","friendly"],["dashing","moving quickly"],["contemptuous","showing disrespect"],["roadside","side of the road"],["grove","group of trees"],["vain","proud"],["realistic","true to life"],["cowardly","not brave"],["cutlass","short sword"],["gobble","eat quickly"],["clatter","loud noise"],["tickled","touched to make laugh"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Tag questions: \"It's a long way, isn't it?\"","Shades of meaning: \"Do you know who is coming?\" – asking; \"You know…\" – emphasis.","Words with similar sounds but different meanings (homophones) – fare/fair.","Poetic devices in Custard: alliteration (\"clashed and clattered\"), simile (\"brave as a barrel full of bears\"), repetition, onomatopoeia (\"Bang! Bang!\")."]},"qa":[["What was Valli's favourite pastime?","Standing in the front doorway of her house and watching the street."],["How much was the bus fare?","Thirty paise one way, sixty paise for a round trip."],["Why does the conductor call Valli \"madam\"?","In fun – she behaved like a grown-up and was confident."],["Why did Valli not get off at the town bus station?","She had no money to spend there and wanted to return before her mother woke up."],["Why did Valli refuse to look out of the window on the way back?","She saw the cow she had laughed at lying dead by the roadside; it made her sad."],["What did Custard do when the pirate came?","He jumped at the pirate and gobbled him up."]],"act":"Collect old bus/train tickets and classify them; write about a memorable journey; discuss road safety rules.","kn":"Ogden Nash (1902–1971) – American poet famous for light, humorous verse.","qz":[["Who wrote \"Madam Rides the Bus\"?","Vallikkannan","Ruskin Bond","Ogden Nash","K.A. Abbas"],["Valli's full name was?","Valliammai","Vallikkannan","Valli Devi","Valliamma"],["How old was Valli?","Eight","Ten","Six","Twelve"],["What was Valli's favourite pastime?","Watching the street from the front door","Reading","Playing with dolls","Singing"],["How far was the town?","Six miles","Ten miles","Three miles","Sixty miles"],["What was the one-way fare?","Thirty paise","Sixty paise","Ten paise","One rupee"],["How long did the journey take?","Forty-five minutes","One hour","Thirty minutes","Two hours"],["The conductor called Valli?","Madam","Baby","Child","Sister"],["What made Valli laugh on the way?","A young cow running in front of the bus","A monkey","A clown","A balloon"],["What did Valli see on the way back?","The cow lying dead","A new building","Her mother","A fair"],["Why didn't Valli want a drink at the stall?","She did not have enough money / was self-respecting","She was not thirsty","She was angry","The stall was closed"],["\"The Tale of Custard the Dragon\" is written by?","Ogden Nash","Robert Frost","Robin Klein","Carolyn Wells"],["Belinda's kitten was called?","Ink","Blink","Mustard","Custard"],["Who cried for a nice safe cage?","Custard","Mustard","Ink","Blink"],["Who defeated the pirate?","Custard","Mustard","Belinda","Ink"],["\"Brave as a barrel full of bears\" is a?","Simile","Metaphor","Personification","Irony"]],"wb":{"mis":"Tag questions: positive statement → negative tag (\"It is late, isn't it?\"); negative statement → positive tag (\"You can't swim, can you?\").","qz":[["\"Valli is brave, ___?\"","isn't she","is she","doesn't she","wasn't she"],["\"You haven't paid, ___?\"","have you","haven't you","did you","are you"],["\"Custard\" rhyme scheme?","aabb","abab","abcb","abba"],["\"Haughtily\" means?","Proudly","Sadly","Quickly","Kindly"],["\"Clashed and clattered\" shows?","Alliteration","Simile","Metaphor","Irony"],["\"Fare\" means?","Money for travel","A festival","Fair weather","Food"]],"pg":"75–90","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":81,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 84)","Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 87)","Oral Comprehension Check 3 (పుట 90)","Thinking about the Text (పుట 92)","Speaking and Writing (పుట 93)","The Tale of Custard the Dragon – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 97)","Let's Begin – Valli's journey (పుట 75) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – Pedalling through the Golden Years (పుట 77) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – Q4–Q6 (పుట 78) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – Going Down Hill on a Bicycle (పుట 80) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – words from \"Madam Rides the Bus\" (పుట 80) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar – phrasal verbs (పుట 81) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – The Sundarbans (పుట 82) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – Mizoram (పుట 84) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – Dialogues (skit) (పుట 85) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – Communicative tasks (samples) (పుట 87) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Can young children travel alone? (paragraph) (పుట 88) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Domestic animals on roads (పుట 88) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Travel tips (పుట 89) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["Oral Comprehension Check 1 (పుట 84)",["1) Valli's favourite pastime was standing in the front doorway of her house and watching what was happening in the street outside.","2) The bus that travelled between her village and the nearest town was a source of unending joy for her. Her strongest desire was to ride on that bus, at least once.","3) She found out that the town was six miles from her village; the fare was thirty paise one way; the trip to the town took forty-five minutes. She learned these details by listening carefully to conversations between her neighbours and people who regularly used the bus, and by asking a few discreet questions.","4) She was planning to take a ride on the bus to the town all by herself, secretly, while her mother slept in the afternoon."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 2 (పుట 87)",["1) The conductor calls Valli \"madam\" in fun, because she behaved like a grown-up, spoke confidently and refused his help.","2) She stands up on the seat because the bus window was covered by a canvas blind and she could not see out; standing up, she saw a canal, palm trees, grassland, distant mountains, the blue sky, and green fields on the other side.","3) When the elderly man called her a child, she said haughtily, \"I'm not a child. I've paid my thirty paise like everyone else.\"","4) She did not want to make friends with the elderly woman because she had big earlobes with huge holes, ugly earrings, and smelled of the betel nut she was chewing; her mouth was full of betel juice – Valli felt disgusted."]],["Oral Comprehension Check 3 (పుట 90)",["1) Valli saved every coin that came her way, resisting the temptation to buy peppermints, toys, balloons; she did not ride the merry-go-round at the village fair. It was not easy – it needed a lot of self-control.","2) A young cow, with its tail high in the air, ran very fast right in front of the bus; the driver honked, it ran faster – Valli laughed till tears came.","3) She had planned to return on the same bus; she had no money to spend in town and wanted to be home before her mother woke up.","4) She refused because she had only thirty paise for the return fare; she was self-respecting and did not want to accept a treat from the conductor. This shows she was proud, determined, independent and careful with money."]],["Thinking about the Text (పుట 92)",["1) Her deepest desire was to ride the bus. Words: \"her strongest desire\", \"unending joy\", \"an overwhelming desire\", \"longing\" to see the world outside.","2) She listened to neighbours, asked discreet questions, found out the fare (30 paise one way), distance (6 miles) and time (45 minutes); she saved every coin and gathered 60 paise; she chose the 1 pm bus when her mother would be sleeping.","3) Valli is curious, intelligent, observant, determined, independent, confident, self-respecting and sensitive. (i) \"Stop the bus! Stop the bus!\" And a tiny hand was raised commandingly. (ii) …still standing outside the bus. (iii) …I've paid my fare. (iv) \"I can manage\" – \"You don't have to help me.\" (v) \"I can take care of myself.\" (vi) …I hope to see you again.","4) He calls her \"madam\" in fun because she behaved like an adult and spoke with authority.","5) \"She clapped her hands with glee\", \"Valli laughed and laughed\", \"she was fascinated by everything she saw\", \"she stood on her seat, looking out of the window\".","6) She saw the young cow that had run in front of the bus now lying dead by the roadside; she was shocked and saddened and lost interest in looking out.","7) Her mother and aunt were talking about something she did not understand fully; Valli, keeping her trip a secret, says she was \"just agreeing with what you said about not knowing everything\". It shows she knew something they did not – her secret adventure.","8) She describes things like a child: the bus \"shining new\" with \"a beautiful stripe\", the cow with its tail in the air, the \"fat\" lady, the shops and people \"a big crowd\"; she claps with joy and laughs – all from an eight-year-old's eyes."]],["Speaking and Writing (పుట 93)",["1) Personal: e.g., I planned a surprise birthday gift for my mother without telling anyone; I saved money and bought it.","2) Personal: e.g., my first train journey to Tirupati was unforgettable because…","3) Concerns: over-speeding, drunk driving, not wearing helmets/seat belts, using mobiles while driving, poor roads, overloading. Suggestions: follow traffic rules, wear helmet, use zebra crossings, strict punishment, awareness programmes, good roads."]],["The Tale of Custard the Dragon – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 97)",["1) Belinda (girl), Ink (black kitten), Blink (grey mouse), Mustard (yellow dog), Custard (little pet dragon), and the pirate. Belinda lived in a little white house with a little black kitten (Ink), a little grey mouse (Blink), a little yellow dog (Mustard) and a little pet dragon (Custard).","2) Custard cried for a nice safe cage because he was a coward and afraid. He is called a \"cowardly dragon\" because, though a dragon has big teeth and spikes, he cried for safety.","3) Belinda tickled him unmercifully because he was a coward; she and the others teased and laughed at him.","4) Poetic devices: alliteration (clashed and clattered), simile (as brave as a barrel full of bears), onomatopoeia (Bang! Bang!), repetition, personification, rhyme.","5) Stanza three: Custard had big sharp teeth, spikes on top of him and scales underneath, a mouth like a fireplace, a chimney for a nose, and daggers on his toes.","6) Rhyme scheme: aabb (house – mouse, wagon – dragon).","7) Images: \"mouth like a fireplace\", \"chimney for a nose\", \"realio, trulio little pet dragon\", \"brave as a barrel full of bears\", \"weaving like a snake\" – pictures without saying directly.","8) It is light-hearted – humorous names (Custard, Mustard), funny exaggeration, the coward who turns hero, and the playful rhythm make it funny.","9) Personal – e.g., a song like \"The Ballad of…\" or folk songs that tell stories (Telugu burra katha)."]],["Let's Begin – Valli's journey (పుట 75) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. On the way to town Valli saw a canal with palm trees, grassland, distant mountains and the blue sky; on the other side a deep ditch and green fields. A young cow ran in front of the bus with its tail raised, and Valli laughed till tears came. In town she saw big shops, crowds and decorated buildings.","The return journey was not the same: she saw the same young cow lying dead by the roadside, hit by a vehicle. Her joy disappeared, she felt sad and did not want to look out of the window any more.","2. (Personal) e.g., my first train journey from Gudur to Chennai – the excitement of the crowded platform, the rhythm of the wheels, vendors, fields and rivers seen from the window."]],["Reading I – Pedalling through the Golden Years (పుట 77) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 It means that people of their age group were becoming less active and resting at home after retirement. The author uses it to contrast her and her husband, who took up a new passion – cycling – at that age.","Q2 They had lived all their lives in Dehradun, were used to the cold winds and narrow winding roads, knew the mountain trails, and got admiration and encouragement from family and friends.","Q3 Their love for cycling and the wish to discover the beauty of Uttarakhand – a world they had always been part of but never had time to explore."]],["Reading I – Q4–Q6 (పుట 78) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q4 Cycling is the most thrilling way, because one feels the sunlight through the trees and the cool breeze, and sees breathtaking views of sal forests, mountains and valleys.","Q5 The 100 km trail from Ghanta Ghar in Dehradun through Kimadi, Hathi Paon, Mussoorie and Chaar Dukaan up to George Everest point.","Q6 A cyclist expects steep slopes, sharp curves, endless hills, lush forests and off-road experiences. Yes, the 100 km ride gave them all these experiences."]],["Reading II – Going Down Hill on a Bicycle (పుట 80) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 (c) excitement","Q2 \"O bird, see; see, bird, I fly.\" / \"For a golden moment share / Your feathery life in air!\"","Q3 The boy shares the joy of flying freely through the air like a bird. The golden moment is the short, precious time when he speeds down the hill without pedalling.","Q4 \"Full of bliss\" means full of great happiness. The boy feels that nothing in this happy world can equal the joy of going downhill – this kind of bliss is rare.","Q5 Going downhill on a bicycle is compared with skating; cycling is said to be more thrilling than skating on level ground with steel-shod skates. (Also the bicycle is compared to an \"airy boat\".)","Q6 Every hill ends in a valley, but whoever climbs up with hard work will again enjoy the flight downhill. Yes, \"toil\" means dedication, determination and will power; the reward is the joy and freedom of flying down – success after hard work."]],["Vocabulary – words from \"Madam Rides the Bus\" (పుట 80) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) curious  (b) elaborate  (c) fascinating  (d) tremendous / overwhelming  (e) scarcely"]],["Grammar – phrasal verbs (పుట 81) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) turned on (b) wake up (c) broke down (d) drove off (e) getting out (f) fill in / fill out"]],["Editing – The Sundarbans (పుట 82) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["An exciting boat journey, gliding across the river from Godkhali jetty, will take you to a place of incredible mystic beauty of Nature. The Sundarbans, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is located at the south-eastern tip of the 24 Parganas district, about 110 km from Kolkata. It got its name from one of the mangrove plants known as Sundari (Heritiera minor). The Sundarbans are a part of the world's largest delta, formed by the mighty rivers Ganga, Brahmaputra and Meghna. It is one of the most attractive and alluring places remaining on earth, a truly undiscovered paradise. The Sundarbans is the largest single block of tidal, halophytic mangrove forests in the world. It is the largest tiger reserve and National Park. Apart from being a unique, largest mangrove eco-system of the world, the Sundarbans has the world's largest deltaic mangrove forests and is also home to one of India's most iconic wildlife species – the Royal Bengal Tiger. The Sundarbans also serves as a crucial protective barrier for the inhabitants in and around Kolkata against the floods …","Spellings corrected: crosswise → across, mangrov → mangrove, Sundrbans/sundarbns → Sundarbans, singl → single, reserv → reserve, mangroove → mangrove, Ttger → Tiger, crusial → crucial, inhabitents → inhabitants."]],["Listening – Mizoram (పుట 84) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. blue-green hills; rugged and uneven (heights)","2. (a) human beings (b) a hill (c) the land of the hill-dwellers","3. The festivals (Mim Kut, Chapchar Kut, Pawl Kut) are associated with agricultural activities; the Mizos enjoy them by singing and dancing.","4. Mizo dances are folk and community dances handed down through generations; they express the gay, carefree spirit of the people and are meant for community participation, not for stage performance.","5. (e) They love to dance and not so much to sing. – not true (they love to dance as much as to sing). [(c) \"protective people\" is also not stated in the passage.]"]],["Speaking – Dialogues (skit) (పుట 85) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Conductor (smilingly): Oh, really! Have you got the fare?","Valli (commandingly): I have to go to town. Here is my money – thirty paise.","Valli (smartly): I can get on by myself. You don't have to help me.","Conductor (jokingly): Oh, please don't be angry with me, my fine madam. Here, take this front seat.","A voice (anxiously): Listen, child, you shouldn't stand like that. Sit down.","Valli (haughtily): There is nobody here who is a child. I have paid my thirty paise like everyone else.","Conductor (smilingly): Oh, sir, this is a very grown-up madam. Do you think a mere girl could pay her own fare and travel to the city all alone?","Valli (angrily): I am not a madam. Please remember that. And you haven't yet given me my ticket.","Conductor (mimicking her): I'll remember.","Valli (steadfastly): Because I want to see everything outside – I'll be careful.","A voice (with concern): If you are so young, is it proper to travel alone? Do you know where you are going?","Valli (irritably): I'm not a child, I tell you. I know where I'm going. Don't worry about me."]],["Speaking – Communicative tasks (samples) (పుట 87) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Task I (in the kitchen): A: Excuse me, Amma, can I help you? B: Thank you, I can manage. A: Let me cut the vegetables at least. B: Oh, that would be wonderful. Thank you, dear.","Task II (school celebration): A: The Independence Day celebration was grand, wasn't it? B: Yes, it was. You took part in the drill, didn't you? A: I did. Could you tell me who designed the stage? B: The art teacher did, with the Class IX students. A: They worked hard, didn't they? B: They certainly did."]],["Writing 1 – Can young children travel alone? (paragraph) (పుట 88) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["The concerns of the elderly lady and the conductor are genuine, and they apply to boys as well as girls. Children of eight are innocent and can easily be misled, lost or hurt in traffic. At the same time, children need some freedom to grow confident and independent, like Valli who planned her trip carefully. The best way is to give freedom with safety: children may travel short, familiar routes, preferably with friends; they must know their full address and parents' phone number, the emergency numbers (112, 1098 Childline), road-safety rules and the difference between safe and unsafe strangers. Parents should know where they go. In my opinion, freedom with education and supervision makes children both safe and confident."]],["Writing 2 – Domestic animals on roads (పుట 88) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Domestic animals should not be let loose on roads where vehicles ply. Stray cattle cause accidents, traffic jams and their own painful death, like the young cow in Valli's story. The owner who left it free is mainly responsible, and so is the careless driver who did not slow down; the local authorities also share the blame for not controlling stray cattle. Owners must keep their animals in sheds, panchayats should run cattle pounds and goshalas, and drivers must drive carefully near villages. Protecting animals is everybody's duty."]],["Project – Travel tips (పుట 89) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) Train: book tickets early, carry ID proof, chain-lock luggage, water, light food, keep the PNR. (b) Bus: reach the stop early, keep change, hold handrails, keep bags safe. (c) Boat: wear a life-jacket, do not overload, sit still, avoid travel in bad weather. (d) Motorbike: helmet, licence and documents, check brakes/tyres/fuel, obey speed limits, never use mobile while riding. (e) Trek: good shoes, map, first-aid kit, water, dry food, torch, travel in a group, inform someone at home.","2 B. Why travel with a ticket? A ticket is proof that we paid for the service; travelling without one is illegal and leads to fines. Ticket money helps maintain buses and trains. Reserved tickets also give us a seat, insurance cover and record for refunds. Honest travel shows good citizenship."]]]},{"id":"en7","no":7,"t":"The Sermon at Benares + Fire and Ice (poem)","m":[11],"ty":"Sermon (Prose) + Poem","th":"Life and death, Grief; Desire and Hatred","by":"Betty Renshaw (Values and Voices); Robert Frost (poem)","sum":"Gautama Buddha (Siddhartha), born a prince in north India, was shielded from suffering. At twenty-five he saw a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession and a monk begging for alms; these sights moved him to leave his palace and seek enlightenment. After seven years of wandering he sat under a peepal tree (Bodhi tree) at Bodh Gaya and became enlightened; he preached his first sermon at Benares. Kisa Gotami's only son died; she went from house to house asking for medicine to cure him. People thought she had lost her senses. A man sent her to the Buddha, who asked her to bring a handful of mustard seed from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent or friend. She found that in every house someone had died. Weary and hopeless, she sat by the wayside and watched the lights of the city flicker and go out – she understood that the fate of men is like these lights; she had been selfish in her grief; death is common to all. The Buddha said: mortals are like ripe fruits and earthen vessels; weeping does not bring peace; the wise do not grieve. Poem – Fire and Ice (Robert Frost): some say the world will end in fire (desire), some in ice (hatred); both are great enough to destroy the world.","te":"సిద్ధార్థుడు రాజకుమారుడు; 25వ ఏట రోగి, వృద్ధుడు, శవయాత్ర, సన్యాసిని చూసి సత్యం కోసం రాజభోగాలు విడిచాడు. ఏడేళ్ళ తర్వాత బోధి వృక్షం కింద జ్ఞానోదయం పొంది బుద్ధుడయ్యాడు; మొదటి ఉపన్యాసం (sermon) బెనారస్ (కాశీ) లో. కిసా గౌతమి ఏకైక కొడుకు చనిపోతే ఇంటింటికీ వెళ్ళి మందు అడిగింది. బుద్ధుడు \"ఎవరూ చనిపోని ఇంటి నుండి గుప్పెడు ఆవాలు తీసుకురా\" అన్నాడు. ప్రతి ఇంట్లో ఎవరో ఒకరు చనిపోయారు. నగర దీపాలు వెలిగి ఆరిపోవడం చూసి – మరణం అందరికీ సహజం, తన దుఃఖంలో స్వార్థం ఉందని గ్రహించింది. కవిత: ప్రపంచం అగ్ని (కోరిక) లేదా మంచు (ద్వేషం) తో అంతమవుతుంది.","pts":["Gautama Buddha (563 BC–483 BC) – born Prince Siddhartha Gautama; enlightened under the Bodhi tree at Bodh Gaya; first sermon at Benares (Sarnath).","Four sights: a sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession, a monk begging for alms.","Kisa Gotami – \"a handful of mustard seed from a house where no one has lost a child, husband, parent or friend\".","Lesson: death is inevitable; grief should not be selfish; the wise do not grieve.","Similes: mortals are like ripe fruits (early danger of falling) and earthen vessels (all are broken at last).","Fire and Ice – Robert Frost; fire = desire/greed/passion, ice = hatred/indifference; rhyme scheme aba abc bcb."],"w":[["sermon","religious talk"],["enlightenment","spiritual knowledge"],["alms","charity to the poor"],["wander","travel aimlessly"],["peepal","sacred fig tree"],["bereaved","having lost a loved one"],["mustard seed","small seeds of the mustard plant"],["wayside","side of the road"],["flicker","burn unsteadily"],["extinguished","put out"],["lamentation","expression of sorrow"],["composed","calm"],["mortal","one who must die"],["afflicted","affected by suffering"],["perish","die, be destroyed"],["suffice","be enough"]],"gr":{"t":"Thinking about Language","p":["Joining clauses with semicolons and dashes: \"She has no interest in music; I doubt she will become a singer.\"","Reported speech: question → \"asked\"; statement → \"said/declared\"; here → there; present → past; present continuous → past continuous; respect → \"respectfully\"; pronouns change.","Idioms/phrases from the text: \"draw out the arrow of lamentation\", \"terms of the world\".","Fire and Ice – irony and understatement (\"ice is also great and would suffice\")."]},"qa":[["Where did the Buddha preach his first sermon?","At Benares (Sarnath)."],["What sights moved Siddhartha?","A sick man, an aged man, a funeral procession and a monk begging for alms."],["What did Kisa Gotami ask for the first time?","Medicine to cure her dead son."],["What did the Buddha ask her to bring?","A handful of mustard seed from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent or friend."],["What did Kisa Gotami understand at last?","Death is common to all; she was selfish in her grief."],["What do fire and ice stand for in Frost's poem?","Fire – desire; ice – hatred."]],"act":"Read Kahlil Gibran's \"On Joy and Sorrow\" and Tagore's verses; discuss how people deal with grief; write about a person who taught you patience.","kn":"The Buddha's teachings: Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path. Sarnath is near Varanasi (Benares).","qz":[["Where did the Buddha preach his first sermon?","Benares","Bodh Gaya","Lumbini","Kushinagar"],["Gautama Buddha's original name was?","Siddhartha Gautama","Ashoka","Mahavira","Ananda"],["At what age did Siddhartha see the four sights?","Twenty-five","Twelve","Thirty-five","Sixty"],["Which was NOT among the four sights?","A rich king","A sick man","An aged man","A monk begging"],["Under which tree was the Buddha enlightened?","Peepal (Bodhi) tree","Banyan tree","Neem tree","Mango tree"],["Kisa Gotami first asked for?","Medicine for her dead son","Food","Money","Mustard seeds"],["The Buddha asked her to bring?","A handful of mustard seed","Water from the Ganga","Flowers","Rice"],["The mustard seed had to come from a house where?","No one had died","There was a child","The owner was rich","There was a temple"],["What did Kisa Gotami watch while sitting by the wayside?","The lights of the city flickering and going out","The stars","The river","The birds"],["Mortals are compared to?","Ripe fruits and earthen vessels","Flowers and trees","Rivers","Mountains"],["\"The wise do not ___, knowing the terms of the world.\"","grieve","eat","work","sleep"],["\"Fire and Ice\" is written by?","Robert Frost","Ogden Nash","Adrienne Rich","John Berryman"],["In the poem, fire stands for?","Desire","Hatred","Love","Peace"],["In the poem, ice stands for?","Hatred","Desire","Snow","Water"],["\"Lomov said that he was getting a noise in his ears\" – in direct speech the verb was?","am getting","was getting","got","had got"],["In reported speech, \"here\" changes to?","there","that","then","now"]],"wb":{"mis":"Reported speech: \"asked\" for questions (no question mark), \"said/told\" for statements; tense moves one step back (is → was, went → had gone); \"this\" → \"that\", \"here\" → \"there\".","qz":[["To report a question we use?","asked","said","told","declared"],["\"I went to visit my grandma,\" said Mamta → Mamta said that she ___ to visit her grandma.","had gone","went","goes","has gone"],["\"What's the matter?\" → Chubukov asked him ___.","what the matter was","what is the matter","what was the matter?","that the matter"],["\"My mowers will be there this very day\" → … would be there ___ very day.","that","this","the","those"],["A word denoting respect is reported with?","respectfully","angrily","loudly","quickly"],["\"Afflicted\" means?","Affected by suffering","Very happy","Rich","Lucky"]],"pg":"91–107","f":"books/10/Class10_English_Workbook_Sem1.pdf","p":97,"lb":"వర్క్‌బుక్","ws":["Before You Read (పుట 98)","Thinking about the Text (పుట 100)","Thinking about Language – Semicolons (పుట 101)","Reported Speech – rules (పుట 102)","Fire and Ice – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 106)","Let's Begin 1 – meanings of 'sermon' (పుట 91) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Let's Begin 2 – 'Sermon at Benares' and 'Death Is Nothing At All' (పుట 92) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – The Dalai Lama (Q1–Q4) (పుట 93) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading I – Q5–Q7 (పుట 94) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Reading II – The Ballad of Father Gilligan (పుట 96) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Vocabulary – antonyms and synonyms (పుట 97) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 1 – Kisa Gotami in reported speech (పుట 98) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 2 – The crow and the peacock (reported speech) (పుట 99) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 3 – At the health clinic (పుట 100) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Grammar 4 – said / told / asked (పుట 101) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Editing – The Wild Geese (పుట 102) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Listening – Sanchi Stupa (పుట 104) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Speaking – grief and how to overcome it (పుట 105) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 1 – Helping people in grief (paragraph) (పుట 105) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Writing 2 – Summaries of the two poems (పుట 106) – వర్క్‌బుక్","Project – Jataka tales (పుట 107) – వర్క్‌బుక్"]},"tg":[["Before You Read (పుట 98)",["1) A sermon is a religious/moral talk given by a spiritual teacher. It is different from a lecture (academic) or a talk (informal); it gives moral advice. \"My mother's sermon about getting my work done on time\" – used humorously for long moral advice.","3) The Sermon on the Mount was delivered by Jesus Christ. The sermon at Benares was delivered by Gautama Buddha."]],["Thinking about the Text (పుట 100)",["1) She asked for medicine to cure her dead son. She did not get it because her son was already dead; no medicine can bring the dead back to life – people thought she had lost her senses.","2) The second time she asked for a handful of mustard seed from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent or friend. She did not get it because in every house someone had died – \"the living are few, but the dead are many\".","3) She understood that death is common to all; everyone loses loved ones; she had been selfish in her grief. Yes, this was what the Buddha wanted her to understand – that grief should be overcome and one must accept death as the law of life.","4) The first time she was blinded by grief and could not think. The Buddha cleverly made her go from house to house and discover the truth herself; seeing the city lights flicker and go out, she realised human life is like those lights. He changed her understanding by experience, not by preaching.","5) Selfishness usually means thinking only of oneself. Yes, I agree – she thought only of her own loss, not realising that everyone suffers loss; her grief made her forget that death is universal."]],["Thinking about Language – Semicolons (పుట 101)",["Three simple sentences: \"There is no means by which those who have been born can avoid dying. After reaching old age there is death. Of such a nature are living beings.\" The single sentence with semicolons has a better rhythm and shows the ideas are closely connected."]],["Reported Speech – rules (పుట 102)",["1) asked 2) said / declared / told 3) there 4) past tense 5) past continuous (\"am getting\" → \"was getting\") 6) respectfully 7) he, him, his, her, their (according to the subject/object)"]],["Fire and Ice – Thinking about the Poem (పుట 106)",["1) Yes, some scientists say the world may end – the sun may burst and burn the earth (fire), or it may grow cold and the earth would freeze (ice). Personal views.","2) Fire stands for desire, greed, lust, passion, avarice, conflict, fury, intolerance. Ice stands for hatred, coldness, indifference, rigidity, insensitivity, intolerance, rejection, cruelty.","3) Rhyme scheme: aba abc bcb (fire – desire – fire; ice – twice – suffice). The rhyming of fire/desire and ice/twice/suffice brings out the two contrasting ideas and links them together; the short last line gives emphasis."]],["Let's Begin 1 – meanings of 'sermon' (పుట 91) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) The text belongs to the first context – a talk on a religious/moral subject (the Buddha's teaching on death and grief).","(b) Religious/moral talk: a speech by a saint, a pravachanam in a temple, a church sermon, a Friday khutba.","Admonition/lecture: parents warning us about mobile phones, a teacher scolding a late-comer, a coach's lecture after losing a match.","(c) We say \"Oh, not again!\" when elders repeat the same advice many times – e.g., \"study hard\", \"don't watch TV\" – because we feel bored or think we already know it."]],["Let's Begin 2 – 'Sermon at Benares' and 'Death Is Nothing At All' (పుట 92) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Similarities: both are sermons by religious teachers about death; both console the grieving; both say death is natural and grief should not overpower us.","Differences: The Buddha teaches that death is common to all, so one should accept it and stop lamenting to attain peace (detachment). Holland's poem says death is only \"slipping into the next room\" – the dead person is still near, love continues and we will meet again (continuity of life, hope of reunion). The Buddha uses a story (Kisa Gotami); the poem is a direct speech by the dead person."]],["Reading I – The Dalai Lama (Q1–Q4) (పుట 93) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 We must tackle the root of the problem, which lies in the human mind.","Q2 The ultimate cause lies within the human mind.","Q3 \"It\" refers to intelligence (accompanied by human affection and compassion).","Q4 Because every human action, verbal or physical, even minor, has some motivation; proper motivation leads to good actions."]],["Reading I – Q5–Q7 (పుట 94) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q5 Because if society's moral values become negative, every individual suffers; the intentions of each person shape the interests of society.","Q6 Not a religious sense, but simply having a good and compassionate heart.","Q7 (a) inner strength (b) less fear and less doubt (c) happiness and open-mindedness – more friends in society"]],["Reading II – The Ballad of Father Gilligan (పుట 96) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Q1 In his tiredness he had complained that he had no rest, joy or peace because people kept dying. He immediately felt sorry and asked God to forgive him – it was his tired body that complained, not his true self.","Q2 (b) the passage of time","Q3 He rode very fast without caring for the rocky lanes, marshes or his own safety and comfort, because he wanted to reach the dying man quickly. It does not mean he did not care for others; he did not care about the danger or the hardship of the road.","Q4 (c) He was full of grief and self-condemnation.","Q5 The wife said that the priest had already come and the man had died happily. The priest realised that God had sent an angel in his form while he slept; so he knelt down to thank God.","Q6 God, who controls the planets and stars, had pity on the tired old priest asleep on a chair and sent an angel to comfort the dying man in his place."]],["Vocabulary – antonyms and synonyms (పుట 97) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["1. (a) befitted (b) grief / sorrow (c) lamentation (d) lost (e) hopeless / weary (f) dead (g) troubled","2. (a) vow – promise, pledge, oath, swear","(b) preach – teach, sermonise, advocate, proclaim","(c) preserve – protect, conserve, save, maintain","(d) awaken – wake, rouse, arouse, stir","(e) enlighten – inform, educate, instruct, illuminate","(f) reign – rule, govern, command, prevail"]],["Grammar 1 – Kisa Gotami in reported speech (పుట 98) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Kisa Gotami had an only son and he died. In her grief she carried the dead child to all her neighbours and asked them for medicine. The people said that she had lost her senses and that the boy was dead.","At length, Kisa Gotami met a man who replied to her request that he could not give her medicine for her child, but he knew a physician who could. The girl requested him to tell her who it was. The man told her to go to Sakyamuni, the Buddha.","Kisa Gotami went to the Buddha and cried to him, addressing him as Lord and Master, to give her the medicine that would cure her boy. The Buddha answered that he wanted a handful of mustard-seed. When the girl joyfully promised to get it, the Buddha added that the mustard-seed must be taken from a house where no one had lost a child, husband, parent or friend.","Poor Kisa Gotami went from house to house. The people pitied her and told her to take the mustard-seed. But when she asked whether a son or daughter, a father or mother had died in their family, they answered sadly that the living were few but the dead were many, and requested her not to remind them of their deepest grief."]],["Grammar 2 – The crow and the peacock (reported speech) (పుట 99) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["It is said that it is not how much we have, but how much we enjoy that makes happiness. … One day a crow saw a swan. He thought that the swan was so white and he was so black, and that the swan must be the happiest bird in the world.","The swan replied that he had been feeling that he was the happiest bird around, but he thought the parrot was the happiest bird as it had two colours. The crow then approached the parrot. The parrot said that it had only two colours, but the peacock had multiple colours.","The crow then visited a peacock in the zoo. Addressing him as dear peacock, the crow told him that he was so beautiful, that thousands of people came to see him every day, and that he was the happiest bird on the planet.","The peacock said that he had always thought that he was the most beautiful and happy bird on the planet, but he was entrapped in the zoo. He added that he had realised that the crow was the only bird not kept in a cage, and could happily roam everywhere."]],["Grammar 3 – At the health clinic (పుట 100) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Ashish complained that he felt sleepy all the time.","Neha complained that she got pain in her legs when she ran.","Sonal said that she was fine.","Ritu mentioned that she had had fever the previous week.","Akash asked why he always coughed.","Rehman asked (the doctor) whether he should walk every day to keep himself fit.","Sumi thanked the doctor and said that now she knew the reason for her constant stomach-ache."]],["Grammar 4 – said / told / asked (పుట 101) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) said (b) told (c) tell (d) asked (e) tell (f) asked"]],["Editing – The Wild Geese (పుట 102) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["One day, Prince Siddhartha was going through the royal gardens on his way to the river. A flock of wild geese passed overhead. Devadatta, the Prince's cousin, shot an arrow into their midst and one of the geese fell. It was wounded and fell just in front of Siddhartha. His heart was filled with compassion when he saw the poor bird bleeding profusely. He lifted it and drew the arrow very carefully from its body. He took the bird with him. Devadatta sent a messenger to claim the bird. But Siddhartha refused to give it. He said that it belonged to him because he had saved its life. It did not belong to Devadatta, who had tried to kill it.","Words added: through, to, of, an, and, in, with, the, to, it, it"]],["Listening – Sanchi Stupa (పుట 104) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["(a) The Sanchi Stupa (Great Stupa) in Madhya Pradesh; it portrays Buddhist art and sculpture.","(b) National and foreign tourists, archaeologists and historians.","(c) It was built during the Mauryan period; it was commissioned by Emperor Ashoka in the third century BCE.","(d) The central chamber is inside the huge hemispherical dome; the relics of Lord Buddha are kept in it.","(e) Four ornamental gateways (toranas) facing four directions and a balustrade surrounding the stupa.","(f) (b) events of Lord Buddha's life [the Buddha is shown symbolically – (d) – on them too]"]],["Speaking – grief and how to overcome it (పుట 105) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Reasons: death of a family member or pet, failure in exams, illness or injury, losing a friend, moving to a new place, losing a position (head boy/girl, school team).","Overcoming: talk to family and friends, express feelings (diary, art), keep a daily routine, play and exercise, help others, seek a counsellor or teacher if sadness continues, remember that loss is part of life (as the Buddha taught)."]],["Writing 1 – Helping people in grief (paragraph) (పుట 105) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["People express grief in many ways – some cry, some become silent, some become angry. We should observe them carefully and understand that some may not want help or may not share their feelings at once; we should respect that. Others want to talk about their thoughts and memories; we must listen patiently without judging, and stand by them. Small actions – holding their hand, sitting with them, bringing food, helping with their work – give great comfort. We should let them know that we care and be patient, because healing takes time. Kind words and presence are often the best consolation."]],["Writing 2 – Summaries of the two poems (పుట 106) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Kahlil Gibran – \"Joy and Sorrow\" (The Prophet): Joy and sorrow come from the same source; \"your joy is your sorrow unmasked\". The well that gives laughter was once filled with tears, the lute that soothes us was hollowed with knives, and the wine cup was burned in the oven. The deeper sorrow carves into us, the more joy we can hold. So the two are inseparable and we should accept both.","Tagore: \"Say not in grief that she is no more, but say in thankfulness that she was.\" We should not mourn a death but be thankful for the life that was lived. Death is not putting out the light of life; it is only putting out the lamp because the dawn (a new, higher life) has come."]],["Project – Jataka tales (పుట 107) – వర్క్‌బుక్",["Suggested tales: The Monkey King (Mahakapi), The Golden Deer, The Hare in the Moon (Sasa Jataka), The Tortoise and the Geese, The Wise Quail, The Elephant and the Dog. Rewrite each in your own words or as a comic strip, illustrate and display as a collage."]]]},{"id":"sr1","no":8,"t":"SR 1: A Triumph of Surgery","m":[7],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Pampering, Proper care of pets","by":"James Herriot","sum":"Mrs Pumphrey, a rich lady, loves her dog Tricki and overfeeds him with cream cakes, chocolates and Horlicks. Tricki becomes hugely fat, listless and sick. The narrator, veterinary surgeon James Herriot, advises a strict diet and exercise, but she cannot bear to deny him. Finally he takes Tricki to his surgery for \"treatment\". There he gives Tricki no food for two days, only water; Tricki mixes with the other dogs, plays, exercises and soon becomes lively and healthy. Meanwhile Mrs Pumphrey sends eggs, wine and brandy \"to build him up\", which the vet and his partners enjoy. After two weeks, Tricki is returned in fine shape; Mrs Pumphrey calls it \"a triumph of surgery\". The real cure was diet, exercise and company.","te":"ధనవంతురాలు మిసెస్ పంఫ్రే తన కుక్క ట్రికీకి క్రీమ్ కేకులు, చాక్లెట్లు అతిగా పెట్టి లావుగా, జబ్బుపడేలా చేసింది. పశువైద్యుడు హెరియట్ ట్రికీని తన ఆసుపత్రికి తీసుకెళ్ళి రెండు రోజులు నీళ్ళు మాత్రమే ఇచ్చాడు; ఇతర కుక్కలతో ఆడుకొని ట్రికీ ఆరోగ్యంగా మారింది. ఆమె పంపిన గుడ్లు, బ్రాందీ వైద్యుడు, సహచరులు వాడుకున్నారు. ఆరోగ్యంగా తిరిగి వచ్చిన ట్రికీని చూసి ఆమె \"శస్త్రచికిత్స విజయం\" అంది – నిజానికి అది ఆహార నియంత్రణ, వ్యాయామం వల్లే.","pts":["Author: James Herriot – English veterinary surgeon and writer.","Tricki – Mrs Pumphrey's Pekinese dog; overfed and pampered.","Treatment: no food for two days, only water; exercise and company of other dogs.","Mrs Pumphrey sent fresh eggs (two dozen a day), wine and brandy.","Theme: excess love and pampering harm; proper care needs discipline."],"w":[["bloated","swollen"],["listless","without energy"],["lethargy","tiredness"],["convalescence","period of recovery"],["ravenous","very hungry"],["prostrate","lying flat"],["concoction","mixture"],["indulge","allow to have too much"],["restorative","something that gives strength"],["triumph","great success"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Irony: Mrs Pumphrey calls diet and exercise \"a triumph of surgery\".","Phrasal verbs: build up, cut down, take away."]},"qa":[["Why was Mrs Pumphrey worried about Tricki?","He was very fat, listless, refused food and had bouts of vomiting."],["What was the narrator's treatment?","No food for two days, only water; exercise and play with other dogs."],["Why was the narrator tempted to keep Tricki as a permanent guest?","Because of the eggs, wine and brandy Mrs Pumphrey sent daily."],["Why did Mrs Pumphrey call it \"a triumph of surgery\"?","She thought surgery cured Tricki; actually diet and exercise did."]],"act":"Read James Herriot's dog stories; discuss proper care of pets.","kn":"Veterinary surgeon = animal doctor.","qz":[["Who wrote \"A Triumph of Surgery\"?","James Herriot","Ruskin Bond","H.G. Wells","Robert Arthur"],["Tricki was a?","Dog","Cat","Parrot","Horse"],["Who was Tricki's mistress?","Mrs Pumphrey","Mrs Hall","Mme Loisel","Mrs Herriot"],["Why was Tricki ill?","Overfeeding and lack of exercise","Poison","Old age","Fever"],["How long was Tricki given no food?","Two days","Two weeks","One day","Five days"],["What did Mrs Pumphrey send to \"build him up\"?","Eggs, wine and brandy","Medicines","Cakes","Toys"],["The narrator was a?","Veterinary surgeon","Teacher","Cook","Policeman"],["What cured Tricki?","Diet and exercise","Surgery","Brandy","Injections"],["\"Ravenous\" means?","Very hungry","Very angry","Very fat","Very sleepy"],["Mrs Pumphrey called the cure a?","Triumph of surgery","Miracle of God","Magic","Accident"]],"wb":{"pg":"1–7","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":6,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 1)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 3)","Think about It (పుట 6)","Talk about It (పుట 7)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 1)",["• Mrs Pumphrey is worried because Tricki has become hugely fat, listless, bloodshot-eyed; he refuses food and has bouts of vomiting.","• She gives him cream cakes, chocolates, malt and cod-liver oil and Horlicks; she reduces walks. No, she is not wise – overfeeding without exercise is making him ill.","• \"I\" refers to the narrator, James Herriot, the veterinary surgeon."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 3)",["• No, the narrator is not as rich as Mrs Pumphrey.","• He treats the dog sensibly – no food for two days, only water; lets him mix and play with other dogs; he becomes fit.","• Because Mrs Pumphrey sent fresh eggs, bottles of wine and brandy daily – the narrator and his partners enjoyed them.","• She thinks it is \"a triumph of surgery\" because she believes surgery cured him, not knowing it was just diet and exercise."]],["Think about It (పుట 6)",["1) The narrator is a sensible, practical, kind and honest professional; he loves animals and knows the right treatment; he has a sense of humour.","2) Yes and no – he was happy to see his mistress but might miss the freedom and company of other dogs. If Mrs Pumphrey again overfeeds him, he will fall sick again.","3) It seems a real-life episode (Herriot was a real vet), told with humour and some exaggeration."]],["Talk about It (పుట 7)",["1) Mrs Pumphrey is foolishly indulgent. Yes, pampering pets/children with too much food and no discipline harms them.","2) Yes, some parents give in to every demand of their children – junk food, gadgets – which spoils their health and character.","3) (i) As staff, I would advise her politely to give Tricki proper diet and exercise. (ii) As a neighbour, I would tell her about the harm of overfeeding.","4) I would also give honest advice and the right treatment, though I would not keep the eggs and brandy for myself."]]]},{"id":"sr2","no":9,"t":"SR 2: The Thief's Story","m":[8],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Trust, Change of heart","by":"Ruskin Bond","sum":"Hari Singh, a fifteen-year-old thief (narrator), befriends Anil, a struggling writer of twenty-five, and offers to work for him. Anil cannot pay but agrees to feed him and teach him to write and add numbers. Hari cooks badly at first but improves; he steals a little from the shopping money. Anil trusts him. One night Anil brings home six hundred rupees from a publisher. Hari steals the money, planning to take the 10.30 Lucknow train, but at the station he cannot board – he thinks of the trust Anil had in him and the chance to become an educated man. He returns in the rain, puts the damp notes back under the mattress. In the morning Anil gives him a fifty-rupee note, still wet – Anil knows, but says nothing. They will start writing sentences soon.","te":"15 ఏళ్ళ దొంగ హరిసింగ్ రచయిత అనిల్ దగ్గర పనిలో చేరాడు; అనిల్ జీతం ఇవ్వలేకపోయినా భోజనం పెట్టి చదువు నేర్పాడు. ఒక రాత్రి అనిల్ తెచ్చిన 600 రూపాయలు దొంగిలించి లక్నో రైలు ఎక్కబోయి – అనిల్ నమ్మకం, చదువుకుంటే గొప్పవాడినవుతాననే ఆశ గుర్తొచ్చి – వెనక్కి వచ్చి డబ్బు యథాస్థానంలో పెట్టాడు. ఉదయం అనిల్ తడిగా ఉన్న 50 రూపాయల నోటు ఇచ్చాడు – అనిల్‌కు తెలుసు, కానీ ఏమీ అనలేదు. నమ్మకం, ప్రేమ మనిషిని మారుస్తాయి.","pts":["Author: Ruskin Bond.","Narrator: Hari Singh (thief, 15 years old) – uses many names.","Anil – 25, struggling writer; earns by fits and starts.","Amount stolen: 600 rupees; train: 10.30 Lucknow Express.","Theme: trust and kindness can reform a person."],"w":[["fairly successful hand","quite skilled"],["grateful","thankful"],["by fits and starts","irregularly"],["grubby","dirty"],["incentive","encouragement"],["ambition","desire to achieve"],["decent","respectable"],["mattress","bed pad"],["stimulated","excited"],["struggling","facing difficulties"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Irony: the thief is reformed by the trust of the person he robbed.","Narration in first person."]},"qa":[["What is Hari Singh \"a fairly successful hand\" at?","Stealing (being a thief)."],["What did Hari get in return for his work?","Food, and Anil taught him to write his name and add numbers."],["Why did Hari not board the train?","He remembered Anil's trust and his own wish to become educated."],["How did Anil show he knew about the theft?","He gave Hari a fifty-rupee note that was still wet."]],"act":"Read \"He Said It with Arsenic\" (Ruskin Bond); discuss how trust changes people.","kn":"Ruskin Bond – Indian author of British descent; lives in Mussoorie; Sahitya Akademi Award.","qz":[["Who wrote \"The Thief's Story\"?","Ruskin Bond","James Herriot","Robert Arthur","Victor Canning"],["How old was Hari Singh?","Fifteen","Twenty-five","Ten","Thirty"],["Anil was a?","Struggling writer","Policeman","Shopkeeper","Teacher"],["How much money did Hari steal?","Six hundred rupees","Fifty rupees","One thousand rupees","Three hundred rupees"],["Which train did Hari plan to catch?","The Lucknow Express","The Delhi Mail","The Mumbai Express","The Kolkata Mail"],["Where did Hari put the money back?","Under the mattress","In the drawer","In the bank","In a book"],["How did Anil show he knew about the theft?","The note he gave was still wet","He called the police","He scolded Hari","He cried"],["What did Anil teach Hari?","To write his name and add numbers","To cook","To steal","To drive"],["Why did Hari return?","Anil's trust and his wish to be educated","He was caught","He forgot his bag","The train was late"],["\"By fits and starts\" means?","Irregularly","Quickly","Regularly","Never"]],"wb":{"pg":"8–13","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":13,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 8)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 10)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 13)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 8)",["• \"I\" refers to the narrator, Hari Singh, a fifteen-year-old thief.","• He is a fairly successful hand at stealing.","• He gets food and Anil teaches him to write his name and add numbers."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 10)",["• He thinks Anil will be only sad, not because of the loss of money but because of the loss of trust.","• Greedy people show fear; rich people show anger; poor people show acceptance. He knew Anil would only show a touch of sadness.","• Yes, he realises – the fifty-rupee note was still wet, but he does not say anything."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 13)",["Think 1) Hari Singh thinks if he gets an education he could become a big man – a clerk or an educated person. He is excited by the prospect and decides not to steal, returning the money.","Think 2) Anil does not hand him over because he understands the boy has repented and returned the money; he wants to reform him with love and trust. Most employers would call the police.","Talk 1) No, such people exist in real life too – kind employers who trust and reform, and young offenders who change.","Talk 2) Yes – being poor himself, Anil understands Hari; the loss would hurt him, yet he forgives; it makes his kindness greater.","Talk 3) Personal answer.","Talk 4) The story is set in a north Indian town (Uttar Pradesh – Lucknow, Hindi names, Muslim and Hindu names)."]]]},{"id":"sr3","no":10,"t":"SR 3: The Midnight Visitor","m":[9],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Presence of mind","by":"Robert Arthur","sum":"Ausable, a fat secret agent who does not look like one, takes Fowler, a young writer, to his room on the sixth floor of a French hotel. They find Max, a rival spy, waiting with a pistol to take an important paper about missiles. Ausable calmly invents a story: a balcony under the window, and that the police are coming because of his complaint; when there is a knock on the door, he says it is the police. Max, frightened, jumps out of the window onto the \"balcony\" – which does not exist – and falls. The knock was actually the waiter bringing drinks. Ausable's presence of mind saved the paper.","te":"బరువైన, సాధారణంగా కనిపించే రహస్య గూఢచారి ఔసబుల్ తన గదిలో పిస్టల్‌తో ఉన్న ప్రత్యర్థి గూఢచారి మాక్స్‌ను చూశాడు. సమయస్ఫూర్తితో కిటికీ కింద బాల్కనీ ఉందని, పోలీసులు వస్తున్నారని కట్టుకథ చెప్పాడు. తలుపు తట్టగానే భయపడిన మాక్స్ కిటికీ నుండి లేని బాల్కనీ మీదికి దూకి కిందపడ్డాడు. తలుపు తట్టింది వెయిటర్. ఔసబుల్ సమయస్ఫూర్తి ముఖ్యమైన కాగితాన్ని కాపాడింది.","pts":["Author: Robert Arthur.","Ausable – fat secret agent; Fowler – young writer seeking thrills; Max – rival agent.","Room on the sixth floor; paper about new missiles.","Imaginary balcony; knock was the waiter (Henry) with drinks.","Theme: presence of mind."],"w":[["secret agent","spy"],["authentic","real"],["thrill","excitement"],["grim","serious"],["passkey","key opening many doors"],["balcony","platform outside a window"],["espionage","spying"],["gendarmes","French police"],["complaint","formal grumble"],["presence of mind","quick thinking"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Irony: the balcony does not exist.","Suspense built through dialogue."]},"qa":[["How is Ausable different from other agents?","He is fat, speaks with an accent, and is not glamorous or adventurous."],["Who was Fowler?","A young writer who wanted to meet a real secret agent."],["How had Max got in?","With a passkey."],["How did Ausable get rid of Max?","He invented a balcony and police; Max jumped from the window."]],"act":"Discuss what you would do in emergencies (fire in kitchen, choking, electric sparks, accident).","kn":"Presence of mind helps in emergencies.","qz":[["Who wrote \"The Midnight Visitor\"?","Robert Arthur","Victor Canning","H.G. Wells","Ruskin Bond"],["Ausable was?","A fat secret agent","A thin detective","A policeman","A writer"],["Fowler was a?","Young writer","Spy","Waiter","Police officer"],["On which floor was Ausable's room?","Sixth","Third","First","Tenth"],["Max came for?","An important paper about missiles","Money","Jewels","Ausable's gun"],["How did Max get in?","With a passkey","Through the window","By breaking the door","From the balcony"],["What did Ausable say was under the window?","A balcony","A swimming pool","A garden","A car"],["Who knocked at the door?","The waiter with drinks","The police","Fowler's friend","The manager"],["What happened to Max?","He jumped out of the window and fell","He was arrested","He shot Ausable","He ran away"],["The story shows Ausable's?","Presence of mind","Cruelty","Laziness","Fear"]],"wb":{"pg":"14–19","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":19,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 14)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 15)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 18)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 14)",["• Ausable is fat, speaks English with an accent, and has no glamour or mystery; he has a small dingy room – unlike the dashing secret agents of films and books.","• Fowler is a young writer; his first authentic thrill was the knowledge that Max was in Ausable's room with a pistol."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 15)",["• Max got in with a passkey.","• Ausable says Max got in through the balcony – the window opens onto a balcony from the next room (an invented story)."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 18)",["Think 1) Ausable was fat, slow, spoke with an accent, lived in a stuffy room and was not mysterious – nothing like the romantic agents Fowler had read about.","Think 2) He tells Max that the police are coming because he complained about the balcony being used before; when the waiter knocks, Max thinks it is the police and jumps to the \"balcony\".","Think 3) He thought up the plan as soon as he saw Max in his room with a gun (when he entered and switched on the light).","Talk 1) Presence of mind = quick, calm thinking in danger. Examples: a boy saving a child from a well; a pilot landing a plane safely.","Talk 2) Fire – switch off gas, use a wet blanket/sand or extinguisher; choking – back blows/Heimlich manoeuvre; sparking appliance – switch off main power; pedestrian knocked down – give first aid, call 108; heavy rain – stay indoors, keep emergency kit; family member late – call them and friends, inform police if necessary."]]]},{"id":"sr4","no":11,"t":"SR 4: A Question of Trust","m":[9],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Honesty, Irony","by":"Victor Canning","sum":"Horace Danby, a respectable, unmarried locksmith of fifty, loves rare and expensive books; once a year he robs a safe to buy them. He plans to steal jewels from the safe at Shotover Grange while the family is away. Entering through the kitchen, he calms the dog Sherry, opens the safe – but a young lady in red appears, says she is the owner, and asks him to open the safe for her as she has forgotten the combination. Horace, wanting to escape without police trouble, opens the safe with his bare hands (no gloves, as his hay fever made him sneeze). She takes the jewels. Later Horace is arrested because of his fingerprints; the lady was actually a thief who tricked him. In prison he works in the library and says that people cannot be trusted.","te":"తాళాల నిపుణుడు హోరేస్ డాన్బీ అరుదైన పుస్తకాలు కొనడానికి ఏటా ఒక దొంగతనం చేసేవాడు. షాట్‌ఓవర్ గ్రేంజ్‌లో సేఫ్ తెరుస్తుండగా ఎరుపు దుస్తుల్లో ఒక యువతి వచ్చి తాను యజమానురాలినని, కాంబినేషన్ మరిచిపోయానని, సేఫ్ తెరవమని అడిగింది. పోలీసులకు భయపడి చేతి తొడుగులు లేకుండానే తెరిచాడు; ఆమె నగలు తీసుకెళ్ళింది. వేలిముద్రల వల్ల హోరేస్ అరెస్ట్ అయ్యాడు – ఆమె కూడా ఒక దొంగే. దొంగకే మోసం జరిగింది (వ్యంగ్యం).","pts":["Author: Victor Canning.","Horace Danby – 50, locksmith, unmarried, loves rare books; steals once a year.","Shotover Grange; dog Sherry; jewels worth about fifteen thousand pounds.","Hay fever – Horace sneezed, removed his gloves.","Irony: a thief is robbed by another thief; \"a question of trust\"."],"w":[["respectable","honourable"],["locksmith","one who makes/repairs locks"],["safe","strong box"],["hay fever","allergy to pollen"],["meticulous","very careful"],["combination","set of numbers to open a lock"],["culprit","wrongdoer"],["subtle","not obvious"],["falter","make a mistake"],["dummy","imitation"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Irony in title: Horace says nobody can be trusted, though he himself is a thief.","Narrative past tense."]},"qa":[["What did Horace Danby like to collect?","Rare and expensive books."],["Why did he steal every year?","To buy rare books – one robbery a year was enough."],["Who was speaking to Horace in the house?","A young lady in red who pretended to be the owner."],["Who was the real culprit?","The young lady – she was a thief."]],"act":"Discuss: do intentions justify actions?","kn":"Fingerprints help police identify criminals.","qz":[["Who wrote \"A Question of Trust\"?","Victor Canning","Robert Arthur","Ruskin Bond","Guy de Maupassant"],["Horace Danby was a?","Locksmith","Doctor","Writer","Policeman"],["Horace liked to collect?","Rare and expensive books","Stamps","Coins","Paintings"],["How often did Horace steal?","Once a year","Every month","Every week","Never"],["Which house did Horace plan to rob?","Shotover Grange","Pumphrey House","Grand Hotel","Loisel House"],["The dog's name was?","Sherry","Tricki","Mustard","Max"],["The young lady wore?","Red","Blue","White","Black"],["Why did Horace remove his gloves?","He had hay fever and wanted a handkerchief / to sneeze","His hands were hot","The lady asked","He forgot"],["Why was Horace caught?","His fingerprints on the safe","The dog barked","The lady complained","A camera recorded him"],["The young lady was actually?","A thief","The owner","A policewoman","Horace's friend"]],"wb":{"pg":"20–25","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":25,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 20)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 22)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 25)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 20)",["• He likes to collect rare and expensive books.","• He steals every year to buy rare books; one robbery a year gave him enough money for the year."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 22)",["• A young, charming lady dressed in red, who pretends to be the lady of the house, speaks to him.","• The young lady is the real culprit – she was a thief who used Horace to open the safe and took the jewels."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 25)",["Think 1) Yes. I began to suspect when she did not call the police at once, was too calm and friendly with a burglar, and asked him to open the safe instead of calling the family – a real owner would not behave like that.","Think 2) She pretended to be the owner, spoke charmingly, threatened to call the police, said she had forgotten the combination, offered a cigarette, and made him open the safe without gloves.","Think 3) Horace was respectable as a locksmith and kind, but he stole once a year to buy books – so not completely honest.","Think 4) He planned meticulously but faltered when he trusted the lady, and when he removed his gloves because of his hay fever – his fingerprints were left.","Talk 1) He deserved punishment because stealing is wrong; but it is ironic that the real thief escaped.","Talk 2) No, good intentions do not justify wrong actions; I would not steal even for a good cause."]]]},{"id":"sr5","no":12,"t":"SR 5: Footprints without Feet","m":[10],"ty":"Science Fiction (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Misuse of science","by":"H.G. Wells","sum":"Griffin, a brilliant but lawless scientist, discovers a drug that makes the human body invisible. In London two boys see fresh muddy footprints appearing on a doorstep – Griffin was walking barefoot, invisible. He had set fire to his landlord's house in revenge and wandered the streets in winter without clothes. He hides in a London store and steals clothes and food; he robs a theatre company of bandages, dark glasses and a false nose. He goes to Iping and stays at an inn run by Mrs Hall, who finds him eccentric. He robs the clergyman's study. When the landlady and her husband find his empty room and clothes moving, they are terrified. When the constable Jaffers tries to arrest him, Griffin removes his clothes and becomes invisible, knocks Jaffers unconscious and escapes.","te":"ప్రతిభావంతుడైన కానీ చట్టాన్ని గౌరవించని శాస్త్రవేత్త గ్రిఫిన్ శరీరాన్ని అదృశ్యం చేసే మందు కనిపెట్టాడు. లండన్‌లో పాదముద్రలు మాత్రమే కనిపించాయి. ఇంటి యజమానిపై పగతో ఇంటికి నిప్పు పెట్టాడు; దుకాణంలో దుస్తులు, ఆహారం దొంగిలించాడు; నాటక కంపెనీ నుండి బ్యాండేజీలు, నల్ల కళ్ళద్దాలు, కృత్రిమ ముక్కు తీసుకున్నాడు. ఇపింగ్‌లోని సత్రంలో ఉండి, మతగురువు డబ్బు దొంగిలించాడు. పోలీసు జాఫర్స్ అరెస్ట్ చేయబోతే దుస్తులు విప్పి అదృశ్యమై తప్పించుకున్నాడు. శాస్త్రాన్ని దుర్వినియోగం చేయకూడదు.","pts":["Author: H.G. Wells – \"The Invisible Man\".","Griffin – scientist; drug makes the body invisible (like glass/water – transparent).","Footprints in London; set fire to landlord's house.","Iping inn – Mrs Hall; clergyman's study robbed; constable Jaffers.","Theme: science misused for selfish ends is dangerous."],"w":[["invisible","cannot be seen"],["lawless","not obeying law"],["eccentric","odd, unusual"],["transparent","see-through"],["inn","small hotel"],["clergyman","priest"],["constable","policeman"],["bandages","strips of cloth"],["wandering","roaming"],["solitude","being alone"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Reported speech in the narrative.","Science fiction – imaginative use of scientific ideas."]},"qa":[["How did the invisible man first become visible?","He wore clothes, bandages, dark glasses and a false nose."],["Why was he wandering the streets?","He had set fire to his landlord's house and had no clothes or shelter."],["Why did Mrs Hall find him eccentric?","He wanted solitude, was rude, never showed his face, and had odd appearance."],["How did Griffin escape from Jaffers?","He removed his clothes and became invisible, then knocked Jaffers unconscious."]],"act":"Discuss: Would you like to be invisible? Read \"The Invisible Man\".","kn":"Transparent objects let light pass through; invisibility means the body neither reflects nor absorbs light.","qz":[["Who wrote \"Footprints without Feet\"?","H.G. Wells","Victor Canning","Robert Arthur","James Herriot"],["Griffin was a?","Scientist","Thief by profession","Doctor","Policeman"],["What did the boys see in London?","Fresh muddy footprints","A ghost","A dog","Clothes flying"],["Why did Griffin set fire to the house?","Revenge on his landlord","By accident","For warmth","To test his drug"],["Where did Griffin hide in London?","A big store","A theatre","A church","A school"],["From the theatre company he took?","Bandages, dark glasses and a false nose","Money","Food","A gun"],["Who ran the inn at Iping?","Mrs Hall","Mrs Pumphrey","Mme Loisel","Mrs Kuperus"],["Whose study was robbed?","The clergyman's","The doctor's","The constable's","Mrs Hall's"],["The constable who tried to arrest Griffin?","Jaffers","Max","Fowler","Horace"],["\"Lawless\" means?","Not obeying the law","Very clever","Very rich","Very poor"]],"wb":{"pg":"26–31","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":31,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 26)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 28)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 31)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 26)",["• He became visible by dressing in clothes, bandaging his face, wearing dark glasses, a false nose and a wig.","• He was wandering because he had set fire to his landlord's house in revenge and had to remove his clothes to remain unseen; he had no home or money."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 28)",["• Mrs Hall finds him eccentric because he arrived in winter in a strange get-up, wanted solitude, was rude, kept his face covered and spoke little.","• In the study, the clergyman and his wife heard footsteps and the chink of money; the desk was open, money disappeared, but no one was seen – only a sneeze was heard.","• At the inn, clothes and furniture moved by themselves; a hat flew at Mrs Hall; a chair attacked her; the bedroom was empty with clothes but no guest."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 31)",["Think 1) Griffin was lawless – he set fire to his landlord's house, stole from a store and a theatre, robbed the clergyman, did not pay rent, and attacked the constable.","Think 2) He was a brilliant scientist who made a great discovery, but he misused it for selfish and criminal purposes; he was not a responsible scientist.","Talk 1) Advantages: help in spying for the country, helping people secretly. Disadvantages: loneliness, cold, hunger, danger of misuse, crime.","Talk 2) Yes – magnetism, gravity, air, electricity, radio waves are invisible forces.","Talk 3) Glass and water are transparent because light passes through them without being absorbed or scattered much."]]]},{"id":"sr6","no":13,"t":"SR 6: The Making of a Scientist","m":[11],"ty":"Biography (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Curiosity, Hard work","by":"Robert W. Peterson","sum":"Richard H. Ebright collected butterflies from kindergarten; by the second grade he had collected all twenty-five species around his hometown. His mother encouraged him, bought him microscopes and books. A book \"The Travels of Monarch X\" made him interested in tagging monarch butterflies for Dr Frederick Urquhart. In the seventh grade he did not win at the science fair – he learned that real experiments are needed, not just displays. He then did projects: viceroy butterflies copy monarchs (birds avoid monarchs); gold spots on the monarch pupa produce a hormone needed for the butterfly's full development. He won prizes and worked in an army lab. Later he proposed a theory of how cells read their DNA. He was also a debater, public speaker and canoeist. Qualities: a first-rate mind, curiosity and the will to win for the right reasons.","te":"రిచర్డ్ ఎబ్రైట్ చిన్నతనం నుండే సీతాకోకచిలుకలు సేకరించాడు; తల్లి ప్రోత్సహించి పుస్తకాలు, మైక్రోస్కోప్ ఇచ్చింది. \"The Travels of Monarch X\" పుస్తకం అతని జీవితాన్ని మలుపు తిప్పింది. సైన్స్ ఫెయిర్‌లో ఓడిపోయి నిజమైన ప్రయోగాలు అవసరమని నేర్చుకున్నాడు. వైస్రాయ్ సీతాకోకచిలుకలు మోనార్క్‌లను అనుకరిస్తాయని, ప్యూపాపై బంగారు చుక్కలు హార్మోన్ ఉత్పత్తి చేస్తాయని కనుగొన్నాడు. కణాలు DNA ని ఎలా చదువుతాయనే సిద్ధాంతం ప్రతిపాదించాడు. ఉత్తమ మేధస్సు, జిజ్ఞాస, సరైన కారణాల కోసం గెలవాలనే తపన – శాస్త్రవేత్తను తయారుచేస్తాయి.","pts":["Author: Robert W. Peterson.","Richard H. Ebright – scientist; collected 25 species of butterflies in his hometown (Reading, Pennsylvania).","Book: \"The Travels of Monarch X\" – Dr Frederick A. Urquhart (University of Toronto).","Projects: viceroy mimics monarch; gold spots on pupa – hormone; theory on DNA.","Qualities: first-rate mind, curiosity, will to win for the right reasons."],"w":[["curiosity","desire to know"],["tagging","attaching labels"],["pupa","stage between larva and adult"],["hormone","chemical messenger in body"],["mimic","copy"],["species","kind of living things"],["competitor","rival"],["debater","one who argues in debates"],["turning point","moment of change"],["will to win","determination"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Biographical narration in past tense.","Compound adjectives: first-rate, eighth-grade."]},"qa":[["How did a book become a turning point in Ebright's life?","\"The Travels of Monarch X\" introduced him to tagging butterflies and research."],["How did his mother help him?","She encouraged him, bought him books, a microscope and other equipment, and took him on trips."],["What lesson did he learn when he did not win at the science fair?","Real experiments, not just pretty displays, win."],["What qualities make a scientist?","A first-rate mind, curiosity, and the will to win for the right reasons."]],"act":"Read about Indian scientists (C.V. Raman, Kalam); list things you wonder about and plan an experiment.","kn":"DNA carries genetic information; Ebright's theory appeared in a scientific journal.","qz":[["Who wrote \"The Making of a Scientist\"?","Robert W. Peterson","H.G. Wells","Ruskin Bond","K.A. Abbas"],["Ebright collected?","Butterflies","Stamps","Coins","Books"],["How many butterfly species were around his hometown?","Twenty-five","Ten","Fifty","Hundred"],["Which book was a turning point?","The Travels of Monarch X","The Invisible Man","Gulliver's Travels","The Necklace"],["Who encouraged Ebright the most?","His mother","His father","His uncle","His teacher only"],["Why did he lose the first science fair?","He only displayed slides, did no real experiment","He was late","He cheated","He was ill"],["Viceroy butterflies copy?","Monarch butterflies","Moths","Bees","Birds"],["Gold spots on the monarch pupa produce?","A hormone","Colour","Poison","Light"],["Ebright proposed a theory on how cells read their?","DNA","Blood","Bones","Skin"],["A scientist needs a first-rate mind, curiosity and?","The will to win for the right reasons","Lots of money","A big lab","Good luck"]],"wb":{"pg":"32–38","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":37,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 32)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 34)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 38)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 32)",["• \"The Travels of Monarch X\" told how monarch butterflies migrate to Central America; it invited readers to help study the migration by tagging butterflies – this opened the world of science to him.","• His mother encouraged his interest, took him on trips, bought him telescopes, microscopes, cameras, mounting materials and books; she was his only companion and helper."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 34)",["• He learned that a winner must do real experiments, not just make neat displays.","• He studied why viceroy butterflies copy monarchs; studied the gold spots on the monarch pupa (hormone needed for development); grew cells from a monarch's wing; later worked on how cells read DNA.","• A first-rate mind, curiosity, and the will to win for the right reasons."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 38)",["Think 1) By being curious, observing, asking questions, reading, doing real experiments and working hard with the guidance of good mentors.","Think 2) Ebright's theory says cells read the blueprint of DNA, which carries genetic information; it explains how cells develop and work.","Talk 1) Children wonder why the sky is blue, how plants grow, why the moon changes shape; curiosity leads to discoveries.","Talk 2) Personal – e.g., \"Why do leaves change colour?\" – I can find out by reading and experiments."]]]},{"id":"sr7","no":14,"t":"SR 7: The Necklace","m":[11],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Vanity, Honesty","by":"Guy de Maupassant","sum":"Matilda Loisel, a pretty but discontented young woman married to a petty clerk, dreams of luxury. Her husband gets an invitation to a ball given by the Minister of Public Instruction. She weeps for a dress; he gives her four hundred francs saved for a gun. Then she has no jewels; she borrows a diamond necklace from her rich friend Mme Forestier. At the ball she is a great success, but on returning home she finds the necklace lost. They search in vain, buy a similar necklace for thirty-six thousand francs, borrowing money at heavy interest. They dismiss the servant, move to a garret and work hard for ten years to repay the debt. Matilda becomes old and rough. One day she meets Mme Forestier and tells her the story; Mme Forestier reveals that the necklace was false – worth only five hundred francs.","te":"గుమాస్తా భార్య మటిల్డా లోయిసెల్ విలాసాలు కలలు కనేది. మంత్రి ఇచ్చిన విందుకు ఆహ్వానం రాగా, 400 ఫ్రాంకులతో దుస్తులు కొని, స్నేహితురాలు మేడమ్ ఫారెస్టియర్ నుండి వజ్రాల హారం అరువు తెచ్చుకుంది. విందు తర్వాత హారం పోయింది. 36,000 ఫ్రాంకులు అప్పు చేసి కొత్త హారం కొని ఇచ్చారు; పదేళ్ళు కష్టపడి అప్పు తీర్చారు. చివరకు ఆ హారం నకిలీదని, విలువ కేవలం 500 ఫ్రాంకులని తెలిసింది. అసంతృప్తి, అహం, నిజం దాచడం వల్ల జీవితం నాశనమైంది.","pts":["Author: Guy de Maupassant (French).","Matilda (Mme) Loisel; husband M. Loisel – clerk in the Board of Education.","Invitation: ball by the Minister of Public Instruction; 400 francs for dress (saved for a gun).","Necklace borrowed from Mme Forestier; replacement cost 36,000 francs; 10 years of hardship.","Twist: the necklace was false – worth 500 francs."],"w":[["vexation","annoyance"],["ball","a formal dance party"],["garret","small room under the roof"],["usurer","one who lends money at high interest"],["despair","loss of hope"],["admiration","respect, praise"],["ruinous","causing ruin"],["privation","lack of necessities"],["francs","French currency"],["contentment","satisfaction"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Irony: ten years of suffering for a false necklace.","Formal speech reported in past tense."]},"qa":[["Why was Mme Loisel unhappy?","She was pretty but married a poor clerk; she dreamt of luxury and wealth."],["Where did she borrow the necklace?","From her rich friend Mme Forestier."],["How did they replace the necklace?","They bought a similar one for 36,000 francs with borrowed money."],["What was the truth about the necklace?","It was false, worth only 500 francs."]],"act":"Discuss: \"Honesty is the best policy\"; \"We should be content with what life gives us.\"","kn":"Guy de Maupassant is known for surprise endings.","qz":[["Who wrote \"The Necklace\"?","Guy de Maupassant","H.G. Wells","Anton Chekhov","O. Henry"],["Matilda's husband was a?","Clerk","Minister","Jeweller","Soldier"],["Who gave the ball?","The Minister of Public Instruction","The King","Mme Forestier","The Mayor"],["How much money did the husband give for the dress?","Four hundred francs","Five hundred francs","Thirty-six thousand francs","Hundred francs"],["He had saved that money for?","A gun","A house","A car","A trip"],["From whom did Matilda borrow the necklace?","Mme Forestier","Mrs Pumphrey","Mrs Hall","Her mother"],["How much did the new necklace cost?","Thirty-six thousand francs","Five hundred francs","Forty thousand francs","Ten thousand francs"],["How many years did they work to repay?","Ten","Two","Five","Twenty"],["What was the real value of the lost necklace?","Five hundred francs","Thirty-six thousand francs","Forty thousand francs","One thousand francs"],["The main cause of Matilda's ruin was?","Her vanity and not telling the truth","Bad luck only","Her husband","Mme Forestier"]],"wb":{"pg":"39–46","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":44,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 39)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 41)","Read and Find Out 3 (పుట 42)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 46)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 39)",["• Mme Loisel is pretty but discontented; she suffers because she thinks she was born for luxury but married a poor clerk; she has no dresses or jewels.","• Her husband is a simple, loving, content clerk who tries hard to please her; he sacrifices his savings for her dress."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 41)",["• She has no jewels to wear with the dress.","• She borrows a diamond necklace from her friend Mme Forestier."]],["Read and Find Out 3 (పుట 42)",["• They search for the necklace everywhere, inform the police and newspapers; then decide to replace it.","• They buy a similar diamond necklace for thirty-six thousand francs, using his inheritance of eighteen thousand and borrowing the rest at heavy interest."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 46)",["Think 1) Their life changed from comfort to poverty – they dismissed the maid, moved to a garret, did hard work for ten years; Matilda became old, strong and rough.","Think 2) Her vanity and her failure to tell the truth to Mme Forestier. She could have confessed the loss honestly; the friend would have told her it was false.","Think 3) Mme Forestier would have told her the necklace was false; she would not have suffered ten years.","Think 4) I would tell the truth to the owner and ask for time to replace or repay.","Talk 1) The story was originally in French; translated into English – literature is translated so it reaches more readers.","Talk 2) Yes, honesty is the best policy – truth saves us from bigger problems.","Talk 3) Yes, contentment brings peace; greed for luxury leads to trouble."]]]},{"id":"sr8","no":15,"t":"SR 8: Bholi","m":[11],"ty":"Story (Supplementary Reader)","th":"Education, Women's self-respect, Dowry","by":"K.A. Abbas","sum":"Sulekha, called Bholi (simpleton), was the fourth daughter of Numberdar Ramlal. At ten months she fell from a cot and her brain was slightly damaged; at two she had smallpox which left her face pock-marked. She stammered and was neglected and laughed at. When a girls' primary school opened in the village, Ramlal sent Bholi there – thinking no one would marry her anyway. The kind teacher spoke to her gently, encouraged her and gave her a book; Bholi started learning and lost her stammer. Years later, Bishamber, an old, lame widower with grown-up children, agreed to marry her with no dowry. At the wedding, seeing Bholi's pock-marks, he demanded five thousand rupees. Ramlal paid; but Bholi refused to marry such a greedy, mean man, threw the garland away, and said she would serve her parents and teach in the school. Ramlal was proud; the teacher's work was complete.","te":"నంబర్‌దార్ రామ్‌లాల్ నాలుగో కూతురు సులేఖ (భోళి). చిన్నతనంలో మంచం మీద నుండి పడి, మశూచి వచ్చి ముఖంపై మచ్చలు, నత్తి వచ్చాయి; అందరూ ఆమెను నిర్లక్ష్యం చేశారు. ఊరిలో బాలికల పాఠశాల తెరవగానే ఆమెను పంపారు. దయగల ఉపాధ్యాయురాలి ప్రోత్సాహంతో చదువుకుని, నత్తి పోయింది, ఆత్మవిశ్వాసం వచ్చింది. పెద్దవాడు, కుంటివాడైన బిశంబర్ పెళ్ళి సమయంలో ఐదు వేల కట్నం అడిగితే, భోళి ఆ దురాశపరుణ్ణి పెళ్ళి చేసుకోనని ధైర్యంగా తిరస్కరించింది; తల్లిదండ్రులకు సేవ చేస్తూ బడిలో పాఠాలు చెబుతానంది. విద్య మహిళకు ధైర్యాన్ని, ఆత్మగౌరవాన్ని ఇస్తుంది.","pts":["Author: K.A. Abbas (Khwaja Ahmad Abbas).","Bholi's real name: Sulekha; father: Numberdar Ramlal; fourth daughter.","Fell from a cot at ten months; smallpox at two years; stammered.","Bishamber Nath – old, lame grocer, widower; demanded 5000 rupees dowry.","Theme: education empowers girls; against dowry."],"w":[["simpleton","a foolish person"],["pock-marks","scars of smallpox"],["stammer","speak with breaks"],["tehsildar","revenue officer"],["dowry","money paid at marriage"],["lame","unable to walk properly"],["widower","man whose wife has died"],["apprehension","fear"],["vacant","empty"],["garland","string of flowers"]],"gr":{"t":"Language","p":["Name meaning: Sulekha = one who writes well; Bholi = simpleton – irony.","Reported speech in dialogues."]},"qa":[["Why was Bholi's father worried about her?","She had an ugly face (pock-marks), was slow and stammered; nobody would marry her."],["Why was Bholi sent to school?","Her father thought no one would marry her, so let the teachers deal with her."],["Why did the marriage not take place?","Bishamber demanded five thousand rupees dowry; Bholi refused to marry a greedy man."],["How did the teacher help Bholi?","She spoke kindly, encouraged her to speak and read, and gave her confidence."]],"act":"Discuss: Should girls know and assert their rights? Read \"The Brass Gong\".","kn":"Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961 – giving and taking dowry is a crime.","qz":[["Who wrote \"Bholi\"?","K.A. Abbas","Ruskin Bond","Guy de Maupassant","Vallikkannan"],["Bholi's real name was?","Sulekha","Radha","Sheela","Champa"],["Bholi's father was?","Numberdar Ramlal","Bishamber","Tehsildar","A teacher"],["At what age did Bholi get smallpox?","Two years","Ten months","Five years","Ten years"],["Bholi's speech problem was?","Stammering","Deafness","Loud voice","No speech"],["Why was Bholi sent to school?","No one would marry her, so let her study","To become a doctor","To get a prize","Her mother wanted"],["Who changed Bholi's life?","Her teacher","Her father","Bishamber","The Tehsildar"],["Bishamber demanded how much dowry?","Five thousand rupees","Five hundred rupees","Fifty thousand rupees","Nothing"],["Why did Bholi refuse to marry Bishamber?","He was greedy and mean","He was poor","He was young","He was a teacher"],["What did Bholi decide to do?","Serve her parents and teach in the school","Run away","Marry another man","Go to the city"]],"wb":{"pg":"47–57","f":"books/10/Class10_English_SupplementaryReader_Sem1.pdf","p":52,"lb":"Supplementary Reader","ws":["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 47)","Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 48)","Read and Find Out 3 (పుట 51)","Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 55)"]},"tg":[["Read and Find Out 1 (పుట 47)",["• He was worried because Bholi had pock-marks on her face, was slow-witted and stammered; no one would marry her.","• Bholi was sent to school because the Tehsildar wanted the Numberdar to set an example by sending his daughters; Ramlal thought it does not matter for Bholi since nobody would marry her anyway – let the teachers worry."]],["Read and Find Out 2 (పుట 48)",["• Yes. At first she was afraid, but she enjoyed seeing other girls and the colourful pictures; the teacher's kindness made her happy.","• Yes. The teacher spoke softly and kindly, while at home people scolded, laughed at or ignored her."]],["Read and Find Out 3 (పుట 51)",["• They accept because Bishamber is well-off, does not ask for dowry, and they think nobody else would marry Bholi.","• At the wedding, Bishamber saw her pock-marks and demanded five thousand rupees; Ramlal paid, but Bholi refused to marry such a mean, greedy and contemptible coward."]],["Think about It; Talk about It (పుట 55)",["Think 1) She was afraid because she had never been away from home, was treated badly, and thought she was being sent away like the cow to the fair. Her fears were removed when she saw the school, girls and the kind teacher.","Think 2) The teacher spoke kindly, encouraged her to speak, gave her a book and promised she would become \"the most learned in the village\"; she gave her self-confidence and self-respect.","Think 3) At first she agreed because she was obedient and did not want to hurt her parents. Later she refused because Bishamber was greedy and mean; education gave her the courage to stand up for her dignity.","Think 4) \"Bholi\" means simpleton; her real name is Sulekha (one who writes well). The real name at the beginning prepares us for her change from a simpleton into an educated, confident person.","Think 5) Personal – yes, the story shows how education and kindness can change a neglected girl.","Talk 1) Teachers can help by encouraging girls, giving equal treatment and confidence; society must support girls' education.","Talk 2) Yes, girls should know their rights (education, equality, no dowry) and assert them; dowry should be refused.","Talk 3) They spoke in their own language (Hindi/Hindustani); the story is translated into English."]]]}];
